(comment EPRAnalogyControlMt "EPRAnalogyControlMt a microtheory containing analogy control predicates to support
 everyday physical reasoning from sketches.")
(comment QRDerivativeRulesMt "QRDerivativeRulesMt expresses some rules for reasoning about qualitative derivatives.")
(comment MakingNecessaryBackgroundAssumptions "A specialization of DecidingToDoSomething and AssumingSomethingToBeTrue.  Each instance of MakingNecessaryBackgroundAssumptions is an event in which an agent makes background assumptions that are necessary for solving a problem.")
(comment IdentifyingAWholeWithTheSumOfItsParts "A specialization of AnalyzingSomething. Each instance of IdentifyingAWholeWithTheSumOfItsParts is an event in which an agent explicitly equates or identifies a thing with the sum of (certain of) its parts.  An example would be the act of explicitly observing that the territory of the United States is the spatial sum of the territories of each of the fifty states.")
(comment DeterminingTheBestAnswerFromASetOfChoices "A specialization of SolvingAProblem. Each instance of DeterminingTheBestAnswerFromASetOfChoices
is an event in which an agent determines the best answer from a set of choices.")
(comment SolvingAMathematicalEquation "Taking an equation and determining the value of a variable in terms of constants and other variables within the rules of algebra.  The catalyst to creating this unit was skills required effort.")
(comment SubstitutingBindingsForVariables "A specialization of SolvingAProblem. Each instance of
SubstitutingBindingsForVariables is an event in which an agent
substitutes known bindings for variables in a formula.")
(comment CategorizingAPhysicsProblem "A specialization of SolvingAProblem and GroupingThingsMentally. Each instance of CategorizingAPhysicsProblem
is an event in which an agent determines which category a specific
physics problem belongs to.")
(comment SanityCheckingPhysicsProblemSolution "A specialization of SolvingAProblem. Each instance of SanityCheckingPhysicsProblemSolution 
is an event in which an agent checks the results of calculations performed to solve a physics problem to see whether they are reasonable.")
(comment DeterminingSpecificScalarOrVectorValuesFromContext "A specialization of SolvingAProblem. Each instance of DeterminingSpecificScalarOrVectorValuesFromContext 
is an event in which an agent determines the specific values of certain scalars or vectors based on contextual information.
<p>
An example would be determining that the speed at which Bob is travelling is 65 miles per hour based on the contextual information that Bob is travelling at the speed limit and the speed limit is 65 miles per hour.")
(comment WorkedSolutionStep "A specialization of TextualPCW.  Each instance of WorkedSolutionStep is a description of a specific step taken to solve a problem, such as a physics problem, a calculus problem, or a chemistry problem. See WorkedSolution.")
(comment PhysicsWorkedSolution "A specialization of WorkedSolution (q.v.).  Each instance of PhysicsWorkedSolution is a description of a sequence of steps taken to solve a physics problem.")
(comment PhysicsProblem-WorkEnergyTheorem "A specialization of PhysicsProblem.  Each instance of PhysicsProblem-WorkEnergyTheorem is a physics problem involving the work-energy theorem (aka the work-energy principle), i.e. the principle that net work done on an object (in an event) equals the change in the kinetic energy of that object (in that event).")
(comment PhysicsProblem-PotentialAndKineticEnergy "A specialization of PhysicsProblem.  Each instance of PhysicsProblem-PotentialAndKineticEnergy is a physics problem involving the concepts of potential energy and kinetic energy.")
(comment PhysicsProblem-InclinedPlane "A specialization of PhysicsProblem..  Each instance of PhysicsProblem-InclinedPlane is a physics problem involving an inclined plane.")
(comment PhysicsProblem-DistanceVelocity "A specialization of PhysicsProblem.  Each instance of PhysicsProblem-DistanceTime is a description of a distance-velocity problem to solve using knowledge of physics.")
(comment PhysicsProblem-DistanceTime "A specialization of PhysicsProblem.  Each instance of PhysicsProblem-DistanceTime is a description of a distance-time problem to solve using knowledge of physics.")
(comment PhysicsProblem-ConstantAcceleration "A specialization of PhysicsProblem.  Each instance of PhysicsProblem-ConstantAcceleration is a description of a constant acceleration problem to solve using knowledge of physics.")
(comment Falling-Freefall "Elements of Falling-Freefall are events in which the objectMoving is accelerated downward by the force of gravity and is not supported by anything.  Usually such objects move straight down (Down-Directly) but the stronger the wind or more significant the horizontal velocity, the greater the horizontal component of its direction (directionOfTranslation-Throughout).")
(comment TDC-ExpertSolutionGood "A nuSketch case that describes a viable solution to a 
                  TDC problem.")
(comment TDC-ExpertSolutionBad "A nuSketch case that describes a potentially problematic 
                  solution to a TDC problem.")
(comment TDC-ExpertSolution "A nuSketch case that describes one of possibly several
                  solutions to a TDC problem.")
(comment TDC-Problem "A nuSketch case that describes a problem that can be presented
                  to the student.  These problem desscriptions contain the 
                  initial state of a battle scenario (e.g. terrain, known enemy 
                  positions, etc.) along with a description of the student's
                  task.")
(comment TDC-Case "Says that a case was made specifcally for the Tactical 
                  Decision Coach.")
(comment vpQueryConstraint "vpQueryConstraints are used within various vector-processing queries
          such as positionSatisfying and pathSatisfying to specify constraints
          on the problem solving.")
(comment AnalogyInvariant "Something is viewed as a member of AnalogyInvariant 
                           when in a given mapping that entity is transferred 
                           intact from base to target during skolem resolution.")
(comment CoaCritiqueCase "A COA-critique-case can be used to critique a 
                  Course-of-Action diagram.")
(comment CritiquePointBad "The collection of predicates that can be used to describe the
                  main point of a COA critique, if that point is bad.")
(comment CritiquePointGood "The collection of predicates that can be used to describe the
                  main point of a COA critique, if that point is good.")
(comment CritiquePoint "The collection of predicates that can be used to describe the
                  main point of a given COA critique.")
(comment CoaLocationDescriptor "When describing the location of an object in a COA sketch, there are 
          several different ways to describe that location. Instances of 
          CoaLocationDescriptor denote which style to to use.")
(comment MaintainanceEvent "The collection of events the focal characteristic of which 
                  is a lack of change in some aspect.")
(comment MilitaryTerrainFeature "A region that can be used on a military plan")
(comment Flanking-MilitaryManeuver "In a flanking maneuver, a force tries to position itself in a 
                  flanking position relative to the enemy.  A flanking position
                  is one in which the force is so located that the enemy will
                  expose his flanks or lines of communication if he continues
                  his advance.  A position on the flanks of a hostile route of
                  advance is effective if it allows fires into the enemy's
                  flanks or if it compels the enemy to change direction and
                  attack it in force rather than to contain and bypass it.
                  (from FM 100-40, ch.2, p. 31).")
(comment Movement-MilitaryTask "Sometimes all that is known about a unit's task is that they
                  need to move somewhere.  Use this task in such cases.")
(comment MovementToContact-MilitaryTask "Instances are military-tasks in which a unit attempts to
                  find some other unit or area in order to gain or
                  re-establish contact with the enemy.")
(comment Infiltrate-MilitaryTask "Instances are military-tasks in which a unit infiltrates
                  some area controlled by the enemy.")
(comment RemainInReserve-MilitaryTask "Instances are military tasks in which the unit performing 
                  the task remains in reserve in case it is needed later as
                  situations change.")
(comment GuardFlank-MilitaryTask "Instances are military tasks in which the unit performing 
                  the task protects the flank of the units on which the task 
                  is being performed.")
(comment GuardAdvance-MilitaryTask "Instances are military tasks in which the unit performing 
                  the task protects the front of the units on which the task 
                  is being performed.")
(comment Guard-MilitaryTask "A form of security operation whose primary task is to protect 
                  the main force by fighting to gain time while also observing 
                  and reporting information, and to prevent enemy ground 
                  observation of and direct fire against the main body by 
                  reconnoitering, attacking, defending, and delaying. A guard 
                  force normally operates within the range of the main body's 
                  indirect fire weapons. (FM101-5-1). The tactical task of 
                  'guard' is normally performed by a maneuver unit of any 
                  echelon from battalion to a division. (KF 661.4)")
(comment Reinforce-MilitaryTask "Instances are military tasks in which the unit performing 
                  the task joins forces with the unit being acted upon, thus
                  enabling that second unit to perform other defensive or
                  offensive tasks.")
(comment Enabling-MilitaryTask "The collection of military tasks conducted to facilitate
                  other types of military operations.  As such, enabling 
                  tasks are not ends unto themselves.")
(comment Counterattack-MilitaryTask "Instances are military-tasks in which an offensive response
                  to a military-attack is performed.")
(comment SupportArea "This specifies a support area associated with 
                  a course of action.")
(comment BushyTerrain "Terrain region consisting mostly of bushy growth and small,
                  sparse trees.")
(comment OpenRollingTerrain "Open, gently rolling terrain.")
(comment RainForest "Jungly wet, heavily wooded and vegetated areas.")
(comment Desert "Desert-like habitats: hot, dry, little rain. Scrublike flora. 
                  Sandy soil. Of course not indoors by default, not 
                  underwater.")
(comment Swamp "An area characterized by shallow water, vegetation, small 
                  patches of dry land, mud")
(comment MineField "An area of ground containing mines laid with or without
                  pattern. Usually employed as an intentional method to 
                  influence enemy movement by protecting a friendly force 
                  or positioning the enemy to the advantage of a friendly 
                  force.")
(comment NoFireArea "An area in which no fires or effects from fires may occur 
                  except in defense of an imminent attack.")
(comment FireSupportCoordinationLine "A line established to ensure coordination of fires.  
                  Elements may attack targets forward of this line without 
                  coordination.")
(comment CoordinatedFireLine "A line beyond which conventional surface fire support 
                  means may fire at any time within the zone of the 
                  establishing headquarters without additional coordination.")
(comment TargetedAreaOfInterest "A point or area along a particular avenue of approach 
                  where successful interdiction will cause enemy forces 
                  to abandon a course of action or modify it.  It allows
                  acquisition and engagement by friendly forces.  Usually 
                  linked to intended effects on high payoff targets.")
(comment NamedAreaOfInterest "A point or area along a particular avenue of approach
                  through which enemy activity is expected to occur.  
                  Activity or lack of activity helps confirm or deny a
                  particular enemy course of action.")
(comment LineOfDepartureIsLineOfContact "Delineates forward friendly positions as the LD when 
                  they are in direct contact with enemy elements.")
(comment LineOfDeparture "A linear control measure used to coordinate departure 
                  of attack elements.")
(comment LimitOfAdvance "A linear control measure symbolizing an easily recognizable 
                  terrain feature beyond which attacking elements will not 
                  advance.")
(comment InfiltrationLane "A specifed lane on which units will move into or through 
                  an area occupied by friendly or enemy units.  Movement is 
                  normally conducted in the smallest size elements as possible 
                  to avoid detection.")
(comment FinalCoordinationLine "A linear control measure in the form of a line close to 
                  an enemy position used to coordinate lifting and shifting 
                  of fires.  It should be recognizable on the ground.")
(comment PhaseLine "A linear control measure used to limit, coordinate, and
                  synchronize movement of forces.  Usually located along a 
                  recognizable terrain feature.")
(comment EngagementArea "The area where a force will be contained and engaged with 
                  massed fires to be destroyed.")
(comment DirectionOfAttack "A specified path to be followed during an operation.  
                  Should traverse the actual terrain.  Precision more 
                  important than an Axis.")
(comment BattlePosition "An area occupied to defend against an attacking force.")
(comment AxisOfAdvance "A general path followed by a unit during an operation.  
                  Not precise.")
(comment AssemblyAreaPlanned "An area not currently occupied, but planned for use at a 
                  future time.")
(comment AssemblyArea "Area occupied by units before, during, and after 
                  operations.")
(comment MilitaryObjective "Military objectives are depicted as graphical areas on a 
                  COA sketch. They are control measures in the sense that 
                  they provide limitations to the area that the unit acting 
                  on the objective can adhere to.")
(comment PhysicalObstacle "Sometimes we think of obstacles as other than 
                  MilitaryControlMeasures. They further divide into 
                  either manmade or natural.  Think of this in terms 
                  of something that impedes movement as opposed to the 
                  function of the control measure, which is to intentionally 
                  influence movement to gain operational advantage.")
(comment MilitaryBoundedArea "We think this distinction is necessary to identify where 
                  something is located on the COA sketch.  Allows enclosure 
                  of the element to be identified in boundaries, phase-lines, 
                  assembly-areas, etc.")
(comment BoundedArea "This distinction is to allow for areas with specific
                   boundaries, which are often times borders.")
(comment MilitaryBoundary "Military boundaries provide constraints to the units
                  contained within by limiting their maneuver space and 
                  fires. Military boundaries can be the normal unit 
                  boundary as designated with echelon markers, but can 
                  also be a line-of-departure or a limit-of-advance.")
(comment MilitaryControlMeasure "Military control measures are collections of graphical,
                  intangible objects used in COA sketches to convey restraint 
                  to military units.  These control measure symbols can be 
                  found in FM 101-5-1.")
(comment ArrowDirectionAttributeVal "Declares something to be an acceptable value for a direction attribute of 
  an #$ArrowDrawnSymbol.")
(comment ArrowAttributeVal "Declares something to be an acceptable value for an attribute of 
  an #$ArrowDrawnSymbol.")
(comment ArrowDrawnSymbol "Instances of this type are drawn representations of arrows. For example, an 
  #$ArrowDrawnSymbol might be used to point from a number on a graph to an annotation.")
(comment Rotation-CounterClockwise45 "The collection of all rotations which are 45 degrees counter-clockwise.")
(comment Rotation-Clockwise45 "The collection of all rotations which are 45 degrees clockwise.")
(comment Rotation-45 "The collection of all rotations which are 45 degrees in extent.")
(comment ContainedGlyphGroup "A ContainedGlyphGroup is a set of glyphs that is directly contained inside another glyph.  Glyphs that are inside a glyph inside the container are not counted as part of that glyph group, although they could be members of some glyph group farther down in the containment hierarchy.")
(comment ConnectedGlyphGroup "A ConnectedGlyphGroup is a set of glyphs all of which are connected via the RCC8 relationships EC or PO.")
(comment GlyphGraph "A GlyphGraph is a finite discrete graph whose nodes are glyphs and whose arcs indicate the presence of specific relationships between the glyphs.  Used for visual processing of glyphs.")
(comment GlyphGroup "A GlyphGroup is a set of glyphs that has been grouped together via some criteria.")
(comment ShadedPolygon "The collection of two dimensional polygons drawn with some form of
                       internal shading.")
(comment GeometricFigure-LatinCapitalLetterZ "The collection of geometric polyline figures recognizable as the
         latin uppercase letter Z.")
(comment GeometricFigure-LatinCapitalLetterV "The collection of geometric polyline figures recognizable as the
         latin uppercase letter V.")
(comment GeometricFigure-LatinCapitalLetterT "The collection of geometric polyline figures recognizable as the
         latin uppercase letter T.")
(comment GeometricFigure-LatinCapitalLetterI "The collection of geometric polyline figures recognizable as the
         latin uppercase letter I.")
(comment GeometricFigure-LatinCapitalLetterB "The collection of geometric polyline figures recognizable as the
         latin uppercase letter B.")
(comment GeometricFigure-LatinCapitalLetterA "The collection of geometric polyline figures recognizable as the
         latin uppercase letter A.")
(comment Dot "The collection of all two dimensional point shapes with volume.")
(comment HalfCircle "The collection of all two dimensional geometric shapes which are
                    an enclosed half a circle made up of a line and an arc.")
(comment EmptyImage "Set of images with no elements in them.")
(comment NuSketchSegment "Each Instance of NuSketchSegment refers to conceptual ink segment")
(comment NuSketchPoint "Each Instance of NuSketchPoint refers to a specific
sketch-polyline-point.")
(comment CogSketchProblem "CogSketchProblems are problem given to a student using CogSketch to solve.")
(lispProcedureImplementing TimesFn TimesFn-FIRE-evalfn-handler)
(comment TimesFn "TimesFn is n-ary multiplication.")
(lispProcedureImplementing PlusFn PlusFn-FIRE-evalfn-handler)
(comment PlusFn "PlusFn is n-ary addition.")
(lispProcedureImplementing ConcatenateStringsFn ConcatenateStringsFn-FIRE-evalfn-handler)
(comment ConcatenateStringsFn "A variable-arity IndividualDenotingFunction that takes one or more abstract character-strings and (in effect) concatenates them.")
(lispProcedureImplementing TheSet TheSet-FIRE-evalfn-handler)
(comment TheSet "(TheSet <stuff>) refers to the set of <stuff>")
(lispProcedureImplementing TheList TheList-FIRE-evalfn-handler)
(comment TheList "(TheList <stuff>) refers to the list of <stuff>")
(lispProcedureImplementing JoinListsFn JoinListsFn-FIRE-evalfn-handler)
(comment JoinListsFn "(JoinListsFn . ?lists) appends n lists.")
(lispProcedureImplementing ListFn ListFn-FIRE-evalfn-handler)
(comment ListFn "Creates a form thats Cyc's representation of a list from the arguments passed to it, e.g. evaluating (ListFn foo bar) returns (TheList foo bar)")
(comment AztecLanguage "Amerindian language of the Nahuatlan sub-group")
(comment Communism "The ideology held by those persons who are members of the #$CommunistParty.")
(comment Socialism "The socialistic ideology. The socialistic ideology of Bill Jones is an instance of this ideology. We ca view this unit as some sort of prototypical socialistic ideology.")
(comment OrganizationOfAfricanUnity "The international organization founded to provide unity, felowship, and defense of African states")
(comment CityOfWashingtonDC "The capital city (#$CapitalCityOfRegion) of the #$UnitedStatesOfAmerica, seat of its Federal government, which is located in the #$DistrictOfColumbia, a specially created Federal district between the States of Maryland and Virginia.  Currently, the #$CityOfWashingtonDC and the #$DistrictOfColumbia are coextensive.")
(comment CityOfTehranIran "The city of Tehran located in #$Iran.")
(comment Iceland "#$Iceland is a country in the North

Atlantic Ocean, which occupies #$Iceland-TheIsland.")
(comment Wales "The physical and political aspects of the geographical area called #$Wales.")
(comment MilesianSchoolBeliefs "The beliefs common to the various schools and subschools of philosophy before #$Socrates, #$Plato, and #$Aristotle")
(comment PhilosophyModern "The modern (post industrial revolution) study of philosophy; usually performed in the context of university philosophy departments.")
(comment ElectricalEngineeringField "The Field of Electrical Engineering, a subField of Engineering")
(comment Philosophy-Ancient "The ancient study of philosophy done in the Mediterranean basin.")
(comment ThalesPhilosophy "#$Thales' belief that all things are composed of some form of water")
(comment AnaximenesPhilosophy "Anaximenes' belief that all things are composed of some form of air")
(comment AnaximandersPhilosophy "Anaximander's beliefs that everything was composed of an infinite indeterminate substance and in the existence of a cosmic order.")
(comment Odysseus "Odysseus was a Hellenic hero.  His journey home from the Trojan War is recounted in the Odyssey.")
(comment GenericTemporalMt "Mt which is noncommital about the temporal formalism used")
(comment ContainerGMt "General microtheory for containers and containment")
(comment WorldGeographyDualistMt "This is the microtheory which contains information about countries around the world and which allows countries, cities, etc., to be counted as both instances of #$GeographicalRegion as well as of #$GeographicalAgent.  Assertions which do not need treat #$GeographicalAgents as #$GeographicalRegions should be placed in #$WorldGeographyMt.  Rules and other assertions requiring #$GeographicalAgents to be treated as #$GeographicalRegions but not restricted to #$PlanetEarth (i.e. they would be valid for future human habitation on other #$Planets) should be placed in #$DualistGeopoliticalMt.")
(comment MobyDickNovel "Moby-Dick is Melville's novel about whaling and the nature of evil:  a classic of American literature")
(comment JobMt "The #$JobMt should make many assumptions about the circumstances under which an action is performed, the remuneration the performer can expect, the motivations of the performer, etc.  One important assumption is that all actions are performed as part of a job.  So, if we're talking about #$OpeningADoor, we're talking about someone (a #$DoorMan) doing this as part of his/her job, not about someone just opening a door in the course of their normal activities (even if it's done at that person's workplace - it's not actually a part of his/her job).  The service mt makes a different set of assumptions, about the performer of the action being different from the one benefitting, etc.")
(comment Arithmetic "Simple Arithmetic you learned in grammar school, that cashier, bookkeepers, etc. use.")
(comment UTAustinCampus "the Austin campus of the UniversityOfTexas. The physical grounds of the #$UniversityOfTexasAtAustin.")
(comment BuildingMt "Mt with physical info about humanly occupied constructs.")
(comment AmbientConditionsMt "Microtheory of what it feels like in a place, from the point of view of an observer who might be there. This includes temperature, air quality, etc. This is how what weather feels like locally is described. To specify the ambient conditions at a given locale, use #$AmbientConditionsDataMt.  See also #$WeatherProcessMt.")
(comment UniverseDataMt "A #$DataMicrotheory regarding instances of concepts addressed in #$UniverseGeneralMt.")
(comment HumanSocialLifeMt "A microtheory for representing the general (but not necessarily universal) aspects of human relationships.  The focus is mainly on their social/cultural aspects.")
(comment Linguistics "The scientific study of human languages")
(comment ComputerScience "The general study of computational systems and their applications")
(comment Biology "The field of study which concerns living things and systems of living things (other than human society), viewed as physical objects of scientific study.")
(comment UnitedStatesSocialLifeMt "#$UnitedStatesSocialLifeMt is the microtheory for definitional information about the social institutions in the United States; such as assertions about organizations, politics, laws, and professions would belong here.  How the culture views these things belongs in #$MainstreamAmericanCultureMt.")
(comment BaseKB "#$BaseKB is the most general #$Microtheory currently in use.  Assertions in this context are `accessible' from any other #$Microtheory via the #$genlMt relation.  In the partial order of microtheories, all microtheories have access to #$BaseKB.  An assertion which is true here will by default be true in every context.  The `content' of #$BaseKB consists of very general assertions which are expected to be usable in most or all applications of Cyc, as well as Cyc's most fundamental assertions that it uses in inference, and all completely universal, timeless truths.")
(comment BotanyMt "An instance of both #$TheoryMicrotheory and #$GeneralMicrotheory.  The #$BotanyMt contains general information about the kingdom of plants (see the collection #$Plant).")
(comment BiologyMt "An instance of both #$TheoryMicrotheory and #$GeneralMicrotheory.  The #$BiologyMt contains general information about living things (instances of #$BiologicalLivingObject (q.v.)).")
(comment NaivePhysicsMt "An instance of both #$TheoryMicrotheory and #$GeneralMicrotheory.  The #$NaivePhysicsMt contains information about the behavior that physical bodies (instances of #$PartiallyTangible (q.v.)) undergo in a wide variety of physical situations (where the situations in question are usually instances of #$PhysicalEvent (q.v.)).")
(comment Unknown-HLTruthValue "An instance of #$CycHLTruthValue. #$Unknown-HLTruthValue is the attribute of being neither known to be true nor known to be false.")
(comment MonotonicallyFalse "An instance of #$CycHLTruthValue.  #$MonotonicallyFalse is the attribute of being indefeasibly false.")
(comment MonotonicallyTrue "An instance of #$CycHLTruthValue.  #$MonotonicallyTrue is the attribute of being indefeasibly true.")
(comment SetTheFormat "An instance of #$Format (q.v.) that can be specified to hold of a given predicate with respect to a given argument-place (see #$argFormat).  (#$argFormat PRED N #$SetTheFormat) means that, for any particular way of fixing the other arguments to PRED besides the Nth, there might be any number (including zero) of things such that, if taken as the Nth argument, PRED holds of those arguments.  For example, the format of #$biologicalMother's first argument-place is #$SetTheFormat, since a female animal might have any number of offspring.  Unlike the other reified instances of #$Format, #$SetTheFormat actually places no restriction at all on what PRED might or might not hold of with respect to the relevant argument-place.  But one should not infer from this that entry-format assertions using #$SetTheFormat are pointless; for they forestall duplication of effort by serving notice that the entry-format of a given argument-place has previously been considered and #$SetTheFormat was deemed the appropriate format.  #$SetTheFormat is the most commonly-encountered format in the Cyc Knowledge Base.  Contrast with #$SingleEntry.")
(comment NaiveBiologicalDescentMt "A microtheory for representing the general aspects of animal reproduction, ancestry, and descent, more or less exclusively from a biological perspective. It pertains mostly to larger, common-sensically familiar animals like birds, horses, and elephants (not microscopic creatures, worms, etc.). #$BiologicalSocialMt is visible from it.")
(comment ComputerGMt "The general microtheory for information relevant to general computer-specific knowledge.  #$ComputerGMt has several significant specializations: #$ComputerRunningMt contains computer knowledge that is relevant primarily to operating computers; #$ComputerHardwareMt contains knowledge specific to the hardware a computer is composed of;  #$ComputerSoftwareMt contains knowledge specific to software that can run on a computer.")
(comment India "An instance of #$IndependentCountry.  #$India is a country in southern Asia whose territory constitutes most of the #$IndianSubcontinent (q.v.).  #$India is bounded on the north by #$Bhutan, #$Tibet, and #$China-PeoplesRepublic, on the northwest by #$Pakistan, on the east by #$Burma, on the southeast by the #$BayOfBengal, and on the southwest by the #$ArabianSea.")
(comment FinancialTransactionMt "A specialization of #$OrganizationMt and #$QuantityChangeMt. This #$Microtheory defines and describes money (cf. #$MonetaryValue) and its uses. Notable specializations include #$AccountingMt, #$ShoppingGMt and #$ServiceMt.")
(comment ServiceMt "A specialization of #$OrganizationMt and #$FinancialTransactionMt. This #$Microtheory defines and describes #$ServiceEvents of various kinds. It differs from #$JobMt in covering both professional and non-professional services, and in assuming that at least one other person benefits from the work performed.")
(comment ShoppingGMt "A specialization of #$ProductGMt and #$FinancialTransactionMt. This #$Microtheory describes buying activities that involve finding and selecting items to be purchased. Notable specializations include #$BuyingMt, #$RetailOrganizationDataMt and #$WesternBusinessPracticesMt.")
(comment HumanPhysiologyMt "Naive theory of human (i.e. #$HomoSapiens) physiology.")
(comment True "An instance of #$TruthValue (q.v.).  #$True is the logical notion of truth.  That is, the term '#$True' is used as a sentential constant of CycL that is true under every model theoretic interpretation.  For example, (#$booleanResult T/F #$True) means that the result obtained from the true-or-false test T/F is True.  Cf. #$False.")
(comment False "An instance of #$TruthValue (q.v.).  #$False is the logical notion of falsehood.  That is, the term '#$False' is used as a sentential constant of CycL that is false under every model theoretic interpretation.  For example, (#$booleanResult T/F #$False) means that the result obtained from the true-or-false test T/F is False.  Cf. #$True.")
(comment SingleEntry "An instance of #$Format (q.v.) that can be specified to hold of a given predicate with respect to a given argument-place (see #$argFormat).  (#$argFormat PRED N #$SingleEntry) means that, for any particular way of fixing the other arguments to PRED besides the Nth, there is at most one thing such that, if taken as the Nth argument, PRED holds of those arguments.  That is, if the sequences <... ARGN ...> and <... ARGN-PRIME ...> differ at most in their Nth items and both (PRED ... ARGN ...) and (PRED ... ARGN-PRIME ...) hold, then ARGN = ARGN-PRIME (see #$equals).  For example, the format of #$biologicalMother's second argument-place is single-entry, since an animal can only have one biological mother.  A predicate with a single-entry format argument-place is thus a #$StrictlyFunctionalPredicate (q.v.) that is strictly functional with respect to that argument-place (see #$strictlyFunctionalInArgs).  Contrast with #$SetTheFormat.")
(comment RepublicanPartyIdeology "The ideology held by those persons who are members of the #$RepublicanParty-US.")
(comment UnitedNationsOrganization "An instance of #$InternationalOrganizationOfCountries.  The #$UnitedNationsOrganization was founded after World War II, and now has 191 member countries.  The stated goals of the #$UnitedNationsOrganization include maintaining international peace and security, developing friendly relations among its member nations, and promoting human rights.  The #$UnitedNationsOrganization has several suborganizations and specialized agencies, including #$UNGeneralAssembly, #$UNSecurityCouncil, and #$InternationalCourtOfJustice.")
(comment TemporalStuffType "A specialization of #$StuffType (q.v.) whose instances are all and only those collections that are temporally stuff-like. A collection <code>COL</code> is temporally stuff-like just in case every purely temporal part (see #$timeSlices) of an instance of <code>COL</code> is itself an instance of <code>COL</code>, unless it is a temporal part of a temporal granule of <code>COL</code> (see #$granuleOfTemporalStuff).
<p>
More precisely, for a collection <code>COL</code> to be an instance of #$TemporalStuffType it is both a necessary and sufficient condition that for any <code>OBJ1</code> and <code>OBJ2</code> (with <code>OBJ2</code> at or above <code>COL</code>'s temporal granularity level), if <code>(#$isa OBJ1 COL)</code> and <code>(#$timeSlices OBJ1 OBJ2)</code>, then <code>(#$isa OBJ2 COL)</code>.
<p>
Consider #$WalkingOnTwoLegs.  Take an arbitrary instance <code>WALK</code> of this collection (say Miss America 2000's victory walk down the runway and back); and then take an arbitrary time-slice <code>SUBWALK</code> of <code>WALK</code> that subsumes at least one instance of (the #$granuleOfTemporalStuff for #$WalkingOnTwoLegs) #$TakingAStep (say her trip back from the end of the runway).  <code>SUBWALK</code> is itself an instance of #$WalkingOnTwoLegs.  So #$WalkingOnTwoLegs is an instance of #$TemporalStuffType. See #$TemporalObjectType for the disjoint notion of being temporally object-like.")
(comment TemporalObjectType "A specialization of #$ObjectType (q.v.) whose instances are all and only those collections that are temporally object-like. A collection <code>COL</code> is temporally object-like just in case it generally holds that any proper purely temporal parts (see #$timeSlices) of an instance of <code>COL</code> is not itself an instance of <code>COL</code>.  More precisely, a collection <code>COL</code> is an instance of #$TemporalObjectType just in case it satisfies the following: For any distinct <code>OBJ1</code> and <code>OBJ2</code>, if <code>(#$isa OBJ1 COL)</code> and <code>(#$timeSlices OBJ1 OBJ2)</code>, then <i>not</i> <code>(#$isa OBJ2 COL)</code>.
<p>
For example, none of the proper time-slices of a #$CalendarYear is itself a calendar year.  Likewise for #$TakingAStep.  So #$CalendarYear and #$TakingAStep are both instances of #$TemporalObjectType. Conversely, a proper time-slice of a given #$Date might itself be a #$Date (e.g. an hour has minutes and seconds as time-slices); so #$Date is <i>not</i> a temporal-object-type.
<p>
See #$TemporalStuffType for the disjoint notion of being temporally stuff-like.")
(comment WeatherMt "Contains axioms which are relevant or specific to the weather. Since there are many things that are true in both #$AmbientConditionsMt #$WeatherProcessMt and not true or too special purpose for #$BaseKB use, we need this Mt.")
(comment ManufacturingMt "A specialization of #$MaterialsManipulationMt and #$HumanActivitiesMt. This #$Microtheory defines and describes basic #$Manufacturing.")
(comment TheInput "a #$TheTerm used to indicate specific input into a process.  Its first use was in #$ManufacturingMt, where it is iO PartiallTangible, but this could be different in other mts.")
(comment CommercialBuyingMt "This mt discusses events that are involved in a commercial purchase or sale of an item.")
(comment TheSelectingAVendor "The selection of which vendor to buy from, (not just which vendor to shop at.)")
(comment zipCodeForLocation "A binary predicate that relates a given #$ContactLocation to the #$ZipCode-FiveDigit of its address.  (#$zipCodeForLocation LOC ZIP) means that LOC is within the area whose zip code is ZIP.")
(comment Cyccess "Interface for accessing Smartpages via Cyc, allowing actual smartness.")
(disjointWith NeutralTone NegativeTone)
(disjointWith PositiveTone NeutralTone)
(comment NaiveInformationMt "A naive theory of information which assumes that, for a particular information-bearing thing, every agent can get the same information from it. This theory ignores the pragmatic complications of communication, and the like.")
(comment ProductGMt "A microtheory which holds assertions about the features and compatibilities of non-brandname formal products.  Assertions about brand-name products belong in #$BrandNameProductGMt.  Assertions about physical characteristics of products belong in #$ProductPhysicalCharacteristicsMt (or #$BrandNameProductPhysicalCharacteristicsMt as appropriate).  Assertions about how products are used belong in #$ProductUsageMt.  Assertions relating products to instances of #$GeopoliticalEntity belong in #$WorldProductsMt.")
(comment TheObjectNeeded "This #$TheTerm is used in a Need mt, as the focus for describing whatever the #$needingAgent is in need of.")
(comment PerceptionMt "Mt for assertions and assumptions related to animal perception.")
(comment WesternBusinessPracticesMt "A specialization of #$BusinessGMt, #$ShoppingGMt, and #$BasicWesternLegalConceptsMt. This #$Microtheory describes general (capitalist) business practices as transacted in Western industrialized nations (and, increasingly, world-wide).")
(comment SportsMt "The #$GeneralMicrotheory which describes different sports, as instances of either #$DevisedStructuredActivity or #$SportsEvent, including rules about them, equipment used when participating in them, and other generic assertions.  Assertions about individual #$SportsOrganizations or atheletes belong in sports data microtheories such as #$AmericanCollegeSportsOrganizationsDataMt, #$AmericanProfessionalSportsMt, or #$NonUSProfessionalSportsMt")
(comment IndividualHandball "The collection of games of handball.")
(comment SimpleAstronomyMt "What any educated person should know about the Sun, Moon, planets, etc.  See also #$UniverseGeneralMt and #$EarthObservationalAstronomyMt.")
(comment NaiveSpatialMt "This microtheory provides concepts and rules to represent the natural way we reason about spatial relations.  It deals with representing the number of sides about an object (see #$NoteAboutSidedObject) and the conclusions we can draw from these objects being in static or dynamic situations with regards to only themselves or other objects.")
(comment BasicWesternLegalConceptsMt "A microtheory for representing some of the most basic concepts of law, legal conflict, and dispute adjudication in the US, Europe, and other parts of the world heavily influenced by Roman law, English Common Law, and/or the legal principles associated with the ideals of the American and French revolutions (Rights Of Man, etc.).")
(comment AmericanProfessionalSportsMt "A #$Microtheory for describing North American professional sports and teams.")
(comment actsInBusinessCapacityIn "(#$actsInBusinessCapacityIn AGENT ACT) holds just in case AGENT is acting in a business capacity during ACT.")
(comment TheExtendedPrice "price less discount before taxes")
(comment Cricket-TheGame "The #$Game of cricket.")
(comment CommonwealthOfIndependentStates "This instance of #$InternationalOrganizationOfCountries, CIS for short, is a loose confederation of nations, each of which was formerly a republic of the #$UnionOfSovietSocialistRepublics; all such former republics are members (#$internationalOrganizationMemberCountries) of the CIS, with the exceptions of the Baltic States, #$Latvia, #$Lithuania and #$Moldova. According to the CIA WorldFactbook 1998 edition, available at http://www.odci.gov/cia/publications/factbook/, the aim of the CIS is 'to coordinate intercommonwealth relations and to provide a mechanism for the orderly dissolution of the USSR'.")
(comment NaiveDeviceBehaviorMt "Assumes that the functions a thing is capable of are any behaviors it reasonably, pragmatically can do.  Legal, societal, etc., restrictions are irrelevant.   In this mt, for example, it is OK for an apple to be used as a paperweight.  It is not a function of an apple to be used as a moon rocket.   Also carries an assumption that things are in working condition (see also #$ArtifactsInNormalConditionMt) -- they aren't broken or being manufactured. (#$Jenkins, 7/14/99)")
(comment Role "A specialization of #$ObjectPredicate (q.v.) whose instances relate situations to individuals that are \"involved\" in them in various ways.  As such, each #$Role has its first argument-place constrained to (see #$argIsa) instances of some specialization of #$Situation, and its other argument-places constrained to instances of some specialization of #$Individual.  Note that the sort of \"involvement\" a given #$Role attributes to an individual in a situation might be either active or passive (cf. #$doneBy and #$objectActedOn), either central or peripheral (cf. #$primaryObjectMoving and #$assistingAgent), either \"physical\" or \"conceptual\" (cf. #$objectMoving and #$infoTransferred).  Individuals can be \"involved\" in situations in the relevant way without directly participating in the situations.  For example, an agent may be involved in an event which it does not temporally intersect by being <i>affected</i> by it (thus #$affectedAgent is an instance of #$Role).  Important specializations of #$Role are #$BinaryRolePredicate and <i>its</i> specialization #$ActorSlot.")
(comment TheSpecifiedThing "The role of the main, specified thing in any specification.")
(comment TheDeviceMaking "The #$MakingSomething in which #$TheDeviceRole was made.")
(comment TheMaker "The person that directed the #$MakingSomething in which #$TheDeviceRole was made.")
(comment Vegetarianism "The class of #$BeliefSystems that hold that eating meat is bad: unhealthy, immoral, unpleasant, etc.")
(comment UncleSam "Uncle Sam. Fictitious character representing the U.S. (government?)")
(disjointWith SwimSuit WarmClothes)
(disjointWith Size-Generic-L Size-Generic-XL)
(disjointWith Size-Generic-M Size-Generic-L)
(disjointWith Size-Generic-S Size-Generic-M)
(disjointWith Size-Generic-XL Size-Generic-XXL)
(comment WorldMythologyMt "Within this microtheory, all the mythological creatures of the world are  real animals, and all the facts of mythology are true.")
(comment ModernWesternMedicineMt "This mt is to be used for assertions which are true primarily around the year 2000 in the developed Western countries.  It should not be used for assertions which are generally true of human bodies or the field of medicine worldwide.  It is currently only used for medical assertions.")
(comment CityOfAlgiersAlgeria "The city of Algiers located in #$Algeria.")
(comment RightistIdeology "Agents with this ideology advocate traditional, conservative, often authoritarian government; they are resistant to change from established policies and practices.")
(comment NationalistIdeology "Agents with this ideology profess and practice loyalty to their nation of citizenship, and promote its culture, language, and status among nations over that of any other nation.")
(comment Pacifism "Agents holding pacifist beliefs are opposed to violence.  Extreme pacifists are opposed to it in any circumstance.")
(comment Anachronism "An Anachronism is something that is is done, is used, or somehow appears, outside of the time it belongs in.")
(comment Conservation "The ill-defined notion of conserving (something), like resources or wildlife.")
(comment NonConformity "The idea or property of explicitly opposing the popular viewpoint, of not following fads and social trends.")
(comment Ecology "The study of the interrelationships between organisms and their physical environment.")
(comment productBetaTesterForModel "(#$productBetaTesterForModel ?AGENT MODEL) holds just in case AGENT is a product beta tester of MODEL.")
(comment PacificRim "rough area at western edge of the pacific ocean including #$Japan, the Philippines, China, the Koreas, and probably other countries.")
(disjointWith HavingAMeal DoingMath)
(comment NaiveUSLawCOC "A loose naive view of what is within the law and against the law in the UnitedStates.  This code of conduct assumes that actions are performed in the United States, and that people are located in the US.")
(comment TerrestrialFrameOfReferenceMt "This microtheory assumes that directions are meant in relation to the earth.  I.e. `up` follows a line normal to the earth's surface.  Horizontal is parallel to earth's surface.  North is in the direction the compass points.  This microtheory also assumes that the earth and the ground is stationary (except during earthquakes).  It also assumes that tangible objects feel the downward force of gravity.  Every tangible object in this context has a value for #$altitudeAboveSeaLevel.  It also assumes that the distances between all objects is less than half the circumference of the earth, otherwise stating that #$eastOf was a #$TransitiveBinaryPredicate would result in inaccurate conclusions e.g. that El Paso was east of Austin.  This microtheory allows for objects to be at arbitrarily high altitudes.  See #$NearEarthsSurfaceMt for a microtheory which makes stronger assumptions.  A map context would be different than #$TerrestrialFrameOfReferenceMt because 'north' on a map is often the same as 'up'.  'North' on a map, although it depicts 'terrestrial north', only happens to be parallel to 'terrestrial north' when the map is appropriately aligned.")
(comment DrFrankenstein "The protagonist of the eponymous novel by Mary Wollstonecraft Shelley: Baron Professor Doctor von Frankenstein.")
(comment FrankensteinsMonstersBody "The body used by Doctor Frankenstein to make his creature; originally belonged to Johan Dershan.")
(comment USHealthcareMt "This mt clumps together a group of assertions that concern health care in the United States.  It also assumes that the time is the beginning of the twenty-first century and the place is the UnitedStates.  Note that #$ModernWesternMedicineMt is a closely related mt.")
(comment MMDDYYFormat "A format for dates, in which 010195 means January 1, 1995.")
(comment TwoDashPhoneFormat "A format for phone numbers matching XXX-XXX-XXXX .  The area code comes first, then a dash, then 3 digits, a dash, and 4 more digits.")
(comment Guest "An instance of #$HumanCyclist.  A Cyc user logged in as `#$Guest' will be able to browse the Cyc knowledge base to some extent, but will not have full rights to inspect and modify it.")
(comment HumanActivitiesMt "The statements about actions in this microtheory are true regardless of whether the action is performed by a person or by organizations of people.  If an assertion is only true for actions performed by individual persons, it should go in the #$IndividualHumanActivitiesMt or one of the mts for which it is base; if an assertion is only true for actions performed by organizations, it should go in the #$OrganizationMt or one of its related mts.  The #$HumanActivitiesMt also includes the assumption that the people are not innovative in the way they perform the actions (e.g. tools are used for their intended purpose and nothing else, so no one would unlock a door with a credit card).  This microtheory assumes in addition that agents are rational, that they are aware of relevant aspects of their situation (e.g., aware of the capabilities they have for doing what they are doing), and that tools work the way they are supposed to.")
(comment OrganizationMt "This microtheory assumes that organizations can be appropriately thought of as #$IntelligentAgents.")
(comment InformationGMt "A generic time theory of information.")
(comment PharmaceuticalDetailsMt "Microtheory which contains detailed knowledge about drugs and drug products.")
(comment StealingIsIllegal "This law just states that instances of stealing are criminal acts.")
(comment ObjectPhysicalCharacteristicsMt "A microtheory for assertions about the vocabulary for describing the physical features of objects: #$constituents, #$physicalPortions, etc.  The physical features of natural non-biological object types which are defined in #$NaivePhysicsMt may also be described in this microtheory -- for now: these should probably be split out at some point.  The physical features of types of artifacts should be described in #$ProductPhysicalCharacteristicsMt (or a narrower mt).")
(comment BordersMt "This mt just contains a few general assertions about borders.  It is intended to be used as a #$genlMt by the #$GeographyMt and the #$InternationalTrafficMt.")
(comment HumanPerceptionMt "Mt for all assertions and assumptions related to human perception with the five senses: sight, hearing, smell, taste, touch.")
(comment SeventeenthCenturyAD-LastHalfOf "The last half of the 17th century of the Common Era.")
(comment SeventeenthCenturyAD-FirstHalfOf "The first half of the 17th century of the Common Era.")
(comment HumanAilmentMt "This #$TheoryMicrotheory is a specialization of #$AilmentMt. It is used for the axiomatization of specific knowledge about  ailments affecting human physiology.")
(comment Mineralogy "The study of minerals.")
(comment PeopleMt "A #$Microtheory containing mostly almanac-like information about kinds of people.  Information pertaining to individual instances of #$Person should go in #$PeopleDataMt (or #$CyclistsMt, if the person is an active #$HumanCyclist).")
(comment ContiguousUnitedStates "This physical region includes 48 states - all of those on the North American continent with the exception of Alaska.")
(comment LanguageAndWritingSystemMt "An instance of both #$TheoryMicrotheory and #$GeneralMicrotheory.  The #$LanguageAndWritingSystemMt contains general information about languages (both natural and artificial), as well as the writing systems used for those languages.")
(comment NormalPhysicalConditionsMt "The assumptions of this microtheory are that the temperature, pressure, atmospheric conditions, gravity are all what humans would consider 'normal'.")
(comment AilmentMt "This #$TheoryMicrotheory is intended to be the most general microtheory for axiomatizing knowledge about ailment conditions and diseases (see #$AilmentCondition and its specs). It allows reference to microorganisms and rather general biological knowledge. For describing features of an ailment condition specific to human beings (#$HomoSapiens), or if knowledge about human physiology is needed to support an assertion, consider using #$HumanAilmentMt.")
(disjointWith Boiling Freezing)
(comment InformationTerminologyMt "A microtheory which holds the terminology common to both #$InformationGMt and #$NaiveInformationMt.")
(comment HighTech "#$HighTech is connoted by things which seem modern and technical.  It, like anachronism, is a concept that changes with time - yesterdays computers seem old-fashioned, not #$HighTech.")
(comment WorldWideWebStandard "The #$WorldWideWebStandard specifies the protocols and formats for #$WorldWideWeb-DynamicIndexedInfoSource communications.  It includes the #$WebPage-AIS addressing scheme, and a language (an SGML DTD) called HTML (#$HypertextMarkupLanguage) for describing the contents of an information source or document.")
(comment AccountingMt "The Mt for basic accounting. Assumes valuation of assets based on historical cost, rather than replacement value or market value. Most of the accounting concepts are in #$FinancialTransactionMt, but accounting inference should take place in a context which uses these assumptions")
(comment IntervalEntry "An instance of #$Format (q.v.) that can be specified to hold of a given predicate with respect to a given argument-place (see #$argFormat), where the argument-place in question is one constrained (via #$argIsa) to instances of #$ScalarInterval.   (#$argFormat PRED N #$IntervalEntry) means that, for any particular way of fixing the other arguments to PRED besides the Nth, there may be more than one instance of #$ScalarInterval such that, if taken as the Nth argument, PRED holds of those arguments.  However, if there is more than one instance of #$ScalarInterval taken as the Nth argument in such a situation, it must be the case that each of the instances of #$ScalarInterval intersect one another (see #$quantityIntersects).  That is, if the sequences <... ARGN-1 ...>, <... ARGN-2 ...>, ..., <... ARGN-M ...> differ at most in their Nth items and each of (PRED ... ARGN-1 ...), (PRED ... ARGN-2 ...), ..., (PRED ... ARGN-M ...) holds, then for any distinct ARGN-K and ARGN-L, where K and L are greater than or equal to 1, and less than or equal to M, (#$quantityIntersects ARGN-K ARGN-L). For example, the format of #$volumeOfObject's second argument-place is #$IntervalEntry.")
(comment ComputerRunningMt "In this context instances of #$Computer are assumed to be powered, running and operational.")
(comment VeterinaryMedicine "The field of study which deals with the treatment of non-human animals and diseases that affect them.")
(comment CyclistsMt "A #$Microtheory for stating basic information about users of #$Cyc.")
(comment OrganizationDataMt "A microtheory for representing general (almanac-like) information about specific organizations.")
(comment BookkeepingMt "A #$Microtheory for stating basic bookkeeping information regarding the Cyc Knowledge Base, such as assertions using #$myCreator or #$myCreationTime.")
(comment EnglishParaphraseMt "An instance of both #$ParaphraseDataMicrotheory and #$GeneralMicrotheory.  #$EnglishParaphraseMt is the default context used for generating English paraphrases for CycL expressions.  Many of the assertions required for English paraphrase generation, including those with the predicates #$genFormat and #$genPhrase (qq.v.), can be found in this context.")
(comment GermanNaziIdeology "The ideology held by members of the German National Socialist Party")
(comment ModernMilitaryMt "The microtheory containing information about modern military organizations, practices, and equipment.  Information about military weapons is located in the subset #$ModernMilitaryWeaponsMt and military vehicle information is located in #$ModernMilitaryVehiclesMt.  Exceptions occur when information concerning weapons or vehicles should be accessible from both subset microtheories.  In these cases, the assertions are in the #$ModernMilitaryMt.")
(comment BiologicalSocialMt "A microtheory  which assumes the existence of living things in some sort of society.  The society could be as complex as human society, or as simple as microbes in a pond, that interact in order to mate, compete for food, etc. #$OrganizationGMt and #$HumanActivitiesMt are visible from it.")
(comment Microbiology "The field of study concerning living organisms, or their parts or constituents, that are too small to be seen with the naked eye.")
(comment Zoology "The field of study concerned with nonhuman animals, their parts, populations, and social systems, viewed as physical objects of scientific study.")
(comment Botany "The field of study which concerns plants and plant systems, viewed as physical objects of scientific study.  The objects of botanical study are represented in #$Plant and related Cyc constants.")
(comment VertebratePhysiologyMt "An instance of #$TheoryMicrotheory.  The #$VertebratePhysiologyMt contains information about general features of vertebrate physiology (see the collection #$Vertebrate).")
(comment 2001-ASpaceOdyssey-TheNarrative "The narrative account commonly known as '2001 A Space Odyssey' which was principally created by Stanely Kubrik and Arthur C. Clarke.  One expression of this narrative is #$2001-ASpaceOdyssey-TheMovie.")
(comment SNOMED-Information1993 "The information in SNOMED International, Version 3.0, the third edition of the Systematized Nomenclature of Medicine.")
(comment MeSH-Information1997 "The information in the expanded 1997 version of the National Library of Medicine (NLM)'s thesaurus of Medical Subject Headings.")
(comment SNOMED-Information1996 "The information in SNOMED International, Version 3.3, the third edition of the Systematized Nomenclature of Medicine.")
(comment MarchingBandInstrumentType "The collection of musical instruments which by default are found in a marching band. This collection is context-sensitive: marching bands in Lhasa, Ayacucho, Kigali, Rabat, and Ancient Rome (not to mention the Alpha Centauri system) all would have different default instrument types.  In the #$MainstreamAmericanCultureMt this collection includes #$Tuba, #$Flute, #$DrumInstrument, #$Cymbal.  #$Violin and #$Piano-Acoustic are negative exemplars.  Perhaps #$Clarinet should not be an instance since, although there is nothing abnormal about clarinets being found in certain particular marching bands, clarinets are not something which you would find say 80% of the time.")
(comment TransportationMt "This is a microtheory which should contain a body of axioms and constants relevant to transportation broadly construed.  The scope of this mt is broader than most people initially realize since it includes all manner of transportation events (see #$TransportationEvent) including not only 'typical' transportation events such as train transportation, horseback riding, but also blood cells transporting nutrients, dust particles transporting germs etc.. Basically, any axioms dealing with how one object facilitates the motion of another by moving along with it (see definition of #$TransportationEvent) are appropriate assertions for this microtheory.  Currently (March 97) there are no identifiable domain assumptions, however, it seems intuitively obvious that given their number and complexity that axioms about transportation should be modularized into their own microtheory.  Prime exemplars of assertions that should go in here are `#$transporters and #$transportees are disjoint' or `#$passengers are usually in #$SittingPosture.'.  A borderline positive exemplar of an assertion that should go in here is `windsurfing transportees get wet'.  This assertion seems not relevant enough to transportation in general, but rather to specific facts about late 20th C recreation.  Since such an mt does not currently (March 97) exist, it can go here, although, finding/creating a more specific mt should be given high priority.  A negative exemplar assertion would be one that did not mention an instance of #$TransportationEvent or any specialization of #$transporter, #$transportees.")
(comment groupMembersOneOf "(#$groupMembersOneOf GROUP COLLECTION) means that at least one member of GROUP is a instance of COLLECTION.")
(comment LochNessMonster "A large reptile-like creature said to inhabit #$LochNess-Lake in #$Scotland. ")
(comment SetTheoryMt "The assertions in the #$SetTheoryMt describe mathematical sets, or characterize predicates or functions that apply to sets.  (See #$Set-Mathematical.)  Since #$Collections share some basic properties with mathematical sets, Cyc has several predicates and functions having arguments typed to accept either mathematical sets or collections.  Many of the rules in the #$SetTheoryMt are formulated at this level of generality, and so apply to collections as well as to mathematical sets.")
(comment Mongolia "The nation of Mongolia as it has existed throughout time; includes both its physical and its political aspects.")
(comment Yemen "Yemen (the People's Democratic Republic of Yemen) is a small #$IndependentCountry in the #$ArabianPeninsula, south of #$SaudiArabia, west of #$Oman, on the #$RedSea and the #$IndianOcean.  Yemen possesses the island of Socotra off the horn of #$Somalia.  This constant includes both the physical and political aspects of Yemen throughout time.")
(comment Phonology "The subfield of #$Linguistics dealing with the patterning of sound segments in a language")
(comment Syntax "The subfield of #$Linguistics dealing with the way words are combined to form phrases and sentences")
(comment Phonetics "The subfield of #$Linguistics focusing on the production, transmission, and perception of speech sounds")
(comment Morphology "The subfield of #$Linguistics dealing with minimal units of meaning in language")
(comment Semantics "The subfield of linguistics dealing with meaning of language forms")
(comment Pragmatics "The subfield of #$Linguistics dealing with non-truth-conditional aspects of meaning.  This includes but is not limited to deixis, implicature, and conversational conventions.")
(comment Lexicography "The subfield of #$Linguistics that deals with the writing and compiling of dictionaries")
(comment Dialectology "The subfield of #$Linguistics that deals with dialects")
(comment Psycholinguistics "The subfield of #$Linguistics that deals with the relationship between language and the cognitive or behavioral characteristics of those who use it")
(comment Sociolinguistics "The subfield of #$Linguistics dealing with social aspects of language use")
(comment Neurolinguistics "The study of the neurological processes underlying the development and use of language")
(comment Always-TimeInterval "An instance of #$ContinuousTimeInterval.  This is the interval of time that encompasses all time.  Thus, every #$TimeInterval is a #$timeSlices of #$Always-TimeInterval.
<p>
In more general contexts (see #$Microtheory) we remain agnostic as to whether #$Always-TimeInterval has either a beginning or an end.  But note that if it does have a beginning, it begins when time itself begins; and if it has an end, it ends only when time ends completely.")
(comment MathMt "#$MathMt is a #$GeneralMicrotheory in which mathematical axioms about Cyc constant that represent mathematical concepts are placed.  For example this Mt includes rules such as: two #$NegativeNumbers multplied together yield a #$PositiveNumber, and the #$RationalNumbers are dense (for any two, there's a third that lies between them.)")
(comment CityOfAbadanIran "Iranian city near the

Iran-Iraq border and the far Northeast end of the Persian Gulf.  Also

near the Shatt al Arab waterway.")
(comment RuusAlJibal "Ru'us al-Jibal, the northernmost province of #$Oman, overlooking the #$StraitOfHormuz.  It is separated from the main part of #$Oman by the #$UnitedArabEmirates and in particular by #$Fujairah-Emirate.")
(comment PreColumbianEra "The term used by historians to refer to the period in the Americas before the arrival of the Spanish.  The ending date is set at 1492 (first contact), but may be considered to be later since many cultures did not meet the invaders until decades later.")
(comment DualistGeopoliticalMt "The #$DualistGeopoliticalMt assumes that the instances of #$GeopoliticalEntity and #$GeographicalAgent are both #$Organizations and #$GeographicalRegions.  This allows us, in most cases, not to reify the #$physicalExtent of the entity, and just state geographic aspects directly about the agent/region combination. Thus, under this microtheory the #$CityOfMiamiFL can be regarded as both an #$Organization -- it can do things like enter into agreements with other cities -- and a #$Place -- it is a patch of ground with certain physical features, boundaries, etc.  See also #$PhysicalGeographyMt, in which #$GeographicalAgents are not also #$GeographicalRegions.
<p>
This microtheory is for generic rules about how worlds act that have #$GeographicalAgents which are treated as #$GeographicalRegions.  It should not have any assertions directly related to #$PlanetEarth.  To make assertions specifically about #$PlanetEarth, use the more specific #$WorldGeographyDualistMt")
(comment UnitedStatesGeographyMt "An instance of #$DataMicrotheory.  The #$UnitedStatesGeographyMt contains information about the geopolitical subdivisions (for example, states, counties, and cities) of the #$UnitedStatesOfAmerica.  Note that definitional information about natural geographical features of the U.S. (such as rivers and mountains) should go in the  #$UnitedStatesNaturalGeographyMt (q.v.).")
(comment WorldGeographyMt "This context contains information about individual countries, mountains, lakes,  around the world, except for those in the US, which are in their own context.  Major US features will be made visible in the #$WorldGeographyMt, so that the Rocky Mountains are visible but the Davis Mountains are not.  Assertions that treat instances of #$GeopoliticalEntity as #$GeographicalRegions do not belong in #$WorldGeographyMt, but in #$WorldGeographyDualistMt or specializations thereof.")
(disjointWith PartiallyHairy SparselyHairy)
(comment SENSUS-Information1997 "SENSUS is a #$KnowledgeBase, created by the Information Sciences Institute at USC. An earlier version of it was known as Pangloss, and it was developed primarily in order to support machine translation.--OKeefe, Oct 15, 1997")
(comment Today-Indexical "#$Today-Indexical is a special #$CalendarDay which denotes the current date from the perspective of the instantiation of #$CycTheCollection that is currently being run (see #$Cyc).  If one asks (#$indexicalReferent #$Today-Indexical ?X) one will get an answer in which ?X is bound to whatever the date is according to the central processing unit of #$Cyc.  Thus the referent of #$Today-Indexical does not vary with the #$Microtheory in which one asks (#$indexicalReferent #$Today-Indexical ?X).  Instead the referent of #$Today-Indexical varies from day to day.  In contrast see also #$Now-Generally which is not necessarily a #$CalendarDay and does not necessarily change referent as real time marches forward.  See #$Now which is a #$TimePoint analog of #$Today-Indexical.  However, unlike #$Now, #$Today-Indexical does not necessarily temporally subsume #$Now-Generally in #$RealTimeMt.")
(comment GulfCooperationCouncil "The Gulf Cooperation Council (GCC) was established in 1981 as a non-aligned informal collective security arrangement among Persian Gulf countries during the war between Iran and Iraq.")
(comment CIAWorldFactbook1994Mt "This microtheory contains information in the CIA 1994 World Factbook (previously, but no longer, posted on the web at http://www.odci.gov/cia/publications/nsolo/factbook/global.htm).")
(comment IndustryMt "This microtheory contains general knowledge about industries (instances of the collection #$Industry-Localized, and the general types, #$IndustryType).  Here vocabulary is defined and basic facts are stated about various industries' products, activities, infrastructure, and interrelations.")
(comment CityOfDhahranSaudiArabia "The city of Dhahran located in #$SaudiArabia.")
(comment TheFirstCommandment "The first of the #$TheTenCommandments, which orders that the Hebrews shall have no God before Yahweh.")
(comment NoFirstStrike "An instance of #$CodeOfConduct that is part of the #$ModernInternationalRelationsCOC.  This code prescribes that no country should violently attack another unless their opponent has previously attacked them.")
(comment WorldTradeOrganization "The World Trade Organization, founded 1 January 1995, administers WTO trade agreements, provides  a forum for trade negotiations and disputes, monitors national trade policies, and provides assistance for developing countries.")
(comment InternationalOceanLawCOC "#$InternationalOceanLawCOC represents generally those widely shared agreements, common law, and rights that are concerned with the conduct of traffic and commerce in waterways between nations.")
(comment LawOfTheSea-UnitedNationsConvention "#$LawOfTheSea-UnitedNationsConvention represents the agreement developed by the U.N. Law of the Sea Conference, which comprehensively covers maritime travel, commerce, and resource development.  This convention, based on work of the 1982 U.N. Conference on maritime law, is not yet in force, although many nations endorse and abide by many of its provisions.  The United States, for example, is not a signatory to this convention; it supports all elements of the code except those concerned with ocean mineral rights.")
(comment MineralRightsClause-UNLawOfTheSea "This constant represents that portion of LawOfTheSea-UnitedNationsConvention concerned with rights to mining minerals on the ocean floor.  The United States does not endorse this portion of the U.N. Law of the Sea.")
(comment UnitedStatesMaritimeLaw-COC "#$UnitedStatesMaritimeLaw-COC represents the body of law applying to maritime traffic in domestic U.S. waterways.  Note that even intrastate traffic on waterways is subject to Federal control, as U.S. maritime law has developed.")
(comment RasTannurahRefinery "The major refinery-complex located in the Saudi Arabian port city of #$CityOfRasTannurahSaudiArabia, which in turn is located on the #$PersianGulf. Associated with one of Saudi Arabia's primary oil export terminals.")
(comment IslamicSocietyIdeology "#$IslamicSocietyIdeology is a#$PoliticalBeliefSystem associated with agents -- groups, regimes, or individuals -- who advocate the imposition of a fundamentalist interpretation of #$Islam upon the political and/or personal life of others.  See also #$IslamicNationalistIdeology.")
(comment IslamicNationalistIdeology "#$IslamicNationalistIdeology is a kind of of #$PoliticalBeliefSystem associated with agents who advocate the formation of Islamic states in regions inhabited by adherents of #$Islam.  Agents who have an #$IslamicNationalistIdeology may also have an #$IslamicSocietyIdeology.")
(comment ModernMilitaryWeaponsMt "#$ModernMilitaryWeaponsMt is the #$Microtheory that includes basic facts about modern military weapons (since about 1900 A.D.), covering any advanced country or military group, but excluding facts about weapons stored in any more specific #$Microtheory.  Many microtheories which require access to the #$ModernMilitaryWeaponsMt will have it through the #$ModernMilitaryMt, which has access to the #$ModernMilitaryWeaponsMt, the #$MilitaryForceStructureMt, and the #$ModernMilitaryVehiclesMt.")
(comment ModernMilitaryVehiclesMt "This Cyc #$Microtheory contains information about various categories of vehicles designed for use by military organizations or personnel.  As a rule, if a microtheory requires access to the #$ModernMilitaryVehiclesMt, it will have it through the #$ModernMilitaryMt, which has access to the #$ModernMilitaryVehiclesMt, the #$ModernMilitaryWeaponsMt, and the #$MilitaryForceStructureMt.")
(comment JeddahRefinery "An #$OilRefinery located in Jeddah, Saudi Arabia, and controlled by #$SaudiAramco.  Its production capacity is 82,000 barrels per day.")
(comment RabighRefinery "An #$OilRefinery located in Rabigh, Saudi Arabia, and controlled by #$SaudiAramco.  Its production capacity is 290,000 barrels per day.")
(comment RiyadhRefinery "An #$OilRefinery located in Riyadh, Saudi Arabia, and controlled by #$SaudiAramco.  Its production capacity is 140,000 barrels per day.")
(comment SaudiAramcoHQ "Headquarters of the SaudiAramco corporation, located in the city of Dharan.")
(comment SaudiMinistryOfPetroleumHQ "A workplace: the physical headquarters of the Saudi Arabian Ministry of Petroleum and Mineral Resources.")
(comment CommitteeForDefenseOfLegitimateRights "A Saudi Arabian dissident group which heads the country's main opposition movement.")
(comment UNSecurityCouncil "The U.N. Security Council is the organ of the United Nations responsible 
for the maintenance of international peace and security. The Council
may decide on enforcement measures, economic sanctions
(such as trade embargoes), or collective military action.")
(disjointWith MissileSilo GrainSilo)
(comment Judeo-ChristianMt "Beliefs (typically)  held both by members of the Christian faith and religious Jews.")
(disjointWith TransportInvolvingADriver (TransportViaFn TransportationDevice-Unmanned))
(comment EconomyMt "This microtheory contains knowledge about economy.")
(comment CityOfOranAlgeria "The city of Oran located in #$Algeria.")
(comment TheUser "#$TheUser denotes the `current user' of an individual, running Cyc image.  Note that at any moment there may actually be many current users of an image, communicating with it through various sorts of API: HTML-based browser, telnet API connection, etc. #$TheUser serves as a placeholder allowing Cyc to keep track of relevant characteristics (including authorization, sophistication level) of each distinct user.")
(comment CityOfAlJubaylSaudiArabia "The city of Al Jubayl located in #$SaudiArabia.")
(comment CityOfAnnabaAlgeria "The city of Annaba located in #$Algeria.")
(comment CityOfArzewAlgeria "The city of Arzew located in #$Algeria.")
