(comment AristotelianLogic "Traditional logic based on the logical treatises of #$Aristotle and further developed during the Middle Ages.")
(comment EpistemicLogic "The subfield of #$Logic that is concerned with representing arguments involving statements about knowing and believing.")
(comment DeonticLogic "The subfield of #$Logic that is concerned with representing arguments involving obligation and permission.")
(comment ModalLogic "The subfield of #$Logic that is concerned with representing arguments that involve necessity and possibility.")
(comment PropositionalLogic "The subfield of #$StandardLogic that is concerned with representing propositional arguments.")
(comment PredicateLogic "The subfield of #$StandardLogic that is concerned with arguments involving quantification.")
(comment TenseLogic "The subfield of #$Logic that is concerned with representing arguments that involve tensed statements.")
(comment Philosophy "The queen of the sciences.")
(comment PhilosophyOfLogic "The subfield of #$Philosophy that is concerned with the philosophical character of logical systems.")
(comment STITLogic "The logic of 'See To It That' or 'STIT', a subfield of #$Logic that is concerned with representing arguments involving agency and the obligation and ability to cause (see to it that) some condition becomes true.")
(comment WorldHistoryMt "A microtheory for representing events in world history.")
(comment WorldWarIIMt "A microtheory for representing events of #$WorldWarII.")
(comment CommonLawSystem "An instance of #$LegalSystemType. A #$CommonLawSystem is one in which the primary sources of law are judicial precedents, rather than legislative acts.")
(comment CivilLawSystem "An instance of #$LegalSystemType. A #$CivilLawSystem is one in which the primary sources of laws are legislative acts, rather than judicial precedents.")
(comment MovementMt "This microtheory was created to hold high-level rules about movement.  Specifically, this microtheory should contain all #$salientAssertions and other definitional rules about spec's of #$MovementEvent that do not require the #$objectMoving to have special physical properties. For example, this microtheory should contain #$salientAssertions about  #$Movement-Rotation,  #$Movement-TranslationEvent, #$Translation-Periodic, and #$AscendingEvent.  On the other hand, rules about, e.g., #$LocomotionEvent-Animal, #$FrictionlessMotion, #$PullingAnObject, and #$TippingOver should not be directly asserted in this microcrotheory.")
(comment WebSearchEnhancementMt "#$WebSearchEnhancementMt contains information useful to applications that enhance web search.")
(comment DeadTerm "#$DeadTerm is a term used internally by the Cyc inference engine as a placeholder for a reference to a constant or NAT which has been killed but which is somehow erroneously still referenced.")
(comment Psychology-Behavioral "The specialization of #$Psychology that focuses on the analysis of behavioral patterns and their reinforcement or decay.")
(comment WorldProductsMt "A microtheory which holds the assertions about the connections between countries or other instances of #$GeopoliticalEntity and formal products.")
(comment ModernMilitaryTacticsMt "This instance of #$Microtheory is intended to contain doctrinal rules and definitions pertaining to military tactics.  Since much of the information contained here references data about military force structure, vehicles, and weapons, this microtheory has access to the #$ModernMilitaryWeaponsMt #$MilitaryForceStructureMt and the #$ModernMilitaryVehiclesMt.  A number of more specialized tactical microtheories in turn have access to it: each of these contains information derived from a specific source.  Each of the source-specific microtheories is in turn accessed by the #$ModernMilitaryMt which is intended to have access to all aspects of modern military operations.  Plan representation microtheories that specify military COAs (courses of action) will also have access to it.")
(comment ComputereseLexicalMt "This lexical microtheory contains lexical information about words and usages that are limited to topics related to computers.  See also #$CyclishMt for terminology specific to #$Cyc.")
(comment Baku-RussiaPipeline "This is an `early oil' northern route pipeline used to carry oil from the #$CityOfBakuAzerbaijan to #$Russia.  It is not clear where in Russia the pipeline terminates but it most likely Novorossisk, with Grozny as an intermediate point along the pipeline.")
(comment Baku-GeorgiaPipeline "This is an `early oil' western route pipeline  which is under construction and will be used to carry oil from the #$CityOfBakuAzerbaijan to the #$CityOfSupsaGeorgia, located in #$Georgia-TheNation.")
