(comment MIDI "A #$ComputerInterfaceConvention. 'MIDI' stands for Musical Instrument Digital Interface, and is a standard convention for digitally encoded information about music to be sent between electronic devices, as between synthesizers and computers. The information is parameterized according to pitch, duration, intensity, and instrument, and is not a direct representation of sound. This format is commonly used in electronic synthesizers, which create sounds from midi files using sampled recordings of instruments.")
(comment CouncilOfTheBalticSeaStates "An international organization whose goal is to promote cooperation among the Baltic Sea states in the areas of aid to new democratic institutions, economic development, humanitarian aid, energy and the environment, cultural programs and education, and transportation and communication.")
(comment InternationalHydrographicOrganization "An international organization whose goal is to train hydrographic   surveyors and nautical   cartographers to achieve   standardization in nautical charts   and electronic chart displays   provide advice on nautical   cartography and hydrography   develop the sciences in the field   of hydrography and techniques   used for descriptive   oceanography")
(comment Ethics-PhilosophySubField "The branch of #$Philosophy that deals with the study of morals, obligations, and rules.")
(comment Logistics "A field of study concerned with the flow of material and information between suppliers and consumers, usually for the purpose of creating some product or carrying out some task. Important applications include military logistics and transportation logistics.")
(comment Naturism "The belief system whose adherents believe the human body unclothed is            nothing to be ashamed of and are more likely to be seen #$Nude in public            than non-adherents.")
(comment SnowWhiteAndTheSevenDwarves-TheMovie "#$SnowWhiteAndTheSevenDwarves-TheMovie is collection of instances of AnimatedMovie depicting the story 'Snow White and The Seven Dwarves'. Each element of #$SnowWhiteAndTheSevenDwarves-TheMovie is an actual sequence of moving images with sound. (In other words, the images that people watch on their television screens, or on a movie screen at the theater.)")
(comment NWUTrafficabilityMt "This #$ApplicationContext houses CycL versions of the coordinate system representations devised by David Deyoe in the course of his work for NWU on terrain and trafficability.")
(comment NyseKSMt "This microtheory contains information slurped from the New York Stock Exchange web page, at www.nyse.com.")
(comment Baku-CeyhanPipeline "This is a western route pipeline which will be used to carry oil from the #$CityOfBakuAzerbaijan to the #$CityOfCeyhanTurkey.")
(comment ComputerHardwareMt "Contains knowledge specific to the hardware a computer is composed of.")
(comment Geography "The study of the way different regions of the surface of the #$PlanetEarth vary in such aspects as climate, elevation, soil, vegetation, population, land use, industries, and states. Cf. #$Geology.")
(comment HubbleTelescope "The space-based light telescope orbiting the earth.")
(comment Donlon "Jim Donlon, one of the primary sources of information and interpretation for the battlespace project.  Affiliated with U.S. Army War College.")
(comment Genealogy "Genealogy is the study of family descent.")
(comment Feminism-PhilosophySubField "The subfield of #$Philosophy that is informed by questions surrounding women's experiences, their position in society, their treatment under the laws of various countries, and related issues.")
(comment GeneticEngineering "The branch of biology dealing with the splicing, and recombining, of           specific genetic units from the DNA of living organisms: it is used to           modify the existing genetic codes to produce new, or improved, species,           valuable biochemicals, etc.")
(comment BillOfRights-US "The first ten amendments to the #$USConstitution")
(comment ArticlesOfConfederation "The first constitution of the original 13 United States, adopted in 1781 and replaced in 1789 by the Constitution of the United States.")
(comment USConstitution "The constitution of the #$UnitedStatesOfAmerica.")
(comment ChaosTheory "A subfield of Physics which focuses on the study of stochastic movement.")
(comment Paintball-TheGame "The collection of sports events in which people hunt each other and shoot each other with guns which have paint pellets as bullets.")
(comment DucksAndDrakes "All instances of a game in which a flat stone is bounced along the surface of calm water. For the non-game variety, see #$SkippingAStone.")
(comment PaperChase "An outdoor game in which the hares start off on a long run scattering bits of paper (the scent) and the hounds try to catch them before they reach a designated spot")
(comment RingARosy "All instances of a children's game in which the players dance around in a circle and at a given signal all squat")
(comment PrisonersBase "A children's game in which two teams capture opposing players by tagging them and taking them to their own base")
(comment TreasureHunt-TheGame "All instances of a game in which players try to find hidden articles by using a series of clues")
(comment AlternativeMedicine "#$AlternativeMedicine is one of the ways of treating and          preventing illnesses which involves techniques that are         regarded by modern Western medicine as scientifically         unproven or unorthodox (cf. Medicine-FieldOfStudy).          The term ``alternative medicine'' can         encompass a wide range of therapies, including         chiropractic, homeopathy, acupuncture, #$HerbalMedicine,         meditation, biofeedback,         massage therapy, and various new-age therapies such as         guided imagery and naturopathy.")
(comment InstructionsForMakingFn "This function maps a given type of object to the collection of all instructions for making things of that type. (#$InstructionsForMakingFn TEMPORAL-STUFF-TYPE) denotes the specialization of #$Instructions for making instances of TEMPORAL-STUFF-TYPE.  For example, (#$InstructionsForMakingFn #$Beer) returns the collection of all (complete sets of) instructions for making beer.")
(comment IridiumLLC "Iridium LLC, the global satellite telephone and paging company. Commercially active since November 1, 1998.")
(comment AnagramGame "All instances of a game whose object is to form words from a group of randomly chosen letters")
(comment RiddleGame "The collection of events of games in which a puzzle must be solved on the basis of several cues")
(comment Pez-TheCompany "PEZ, the company producer of #$PezCandy. Originally Austrian, it moved to the USA in 1952.")
(comment Biometrics "The branch of #$Biology which deals with its           data statistically and by mathematical analysis.")
(comment DominoesGame "All instances of any of several games played with small rectangular blocks")
(comment DartsGame "An instance of #$Game. An abstract work which specifies how to play a game in which instances of #$Dart are thrown at a dartboard.")
(comment TicTacToe "All instances of a game in which two players alternately put crosses and circles in one of the compartments of a 3-by-3 board; the object is to get a row of three crosses or three circles before the opponent does")
(comment Craps-TheGame "All instances of a gambling game using two dice")
(comment Canasta "All instances of a form of rummy using two decks and four jokers; jokers and dueces are wild; the object is to meld groups of seven of the same rank")
(comment GinRummy "All instances of a form of rummy in which a player can go out if the cards remaining in their hand total less than 10 points")
(comment Rummy "All instances of a card game based on collecting sets and sequences; the winner is the first to meld all their cards")
(comment RussianBank "All instances of a game in which two play solitaire with separate packs.")
(comment DummyWhist "All instances of a form of whist with three players; four hands are dealt with the hand opposite the dealer being face up")
(comment HeartsGame "All instances of a form of whist in which players avoid winning tricks containing hearts or the queen of spades")
(comment Whist "All instances of a card game for four players who form two partnerships; a pack of 52 cards is dealt and each side scores one point for each trick it takes in excess of six")
(comment Snooker "All instances of a form of pool played with 15 red balls and six balls of other colors and a cue ball")
(comment PoolGame "All instances of any of various games played on a pool table having 6 pockets")
(comment BarBilliards "All instances of a British table game in which short cues are used to knock balls into holes that are guarded by wooden pegs; penalties are incurred if the pegs are knocked over")
(comment Billiards "A #$TableGame played on rectangular cloth-covered #$PoolTable (with cushioned edges) in which long tapering cue sticks are used to propel ivory (or composition) #$PoolBalls")
(comment FederalCommunicationsCommission "The mission of this independent government agency is to encourage competition in all communications markets and to protect the public interest. In response to direction from the Congress, the FCC develops and implements policy concerning interstate and international communications by radio, television, wire, satellite, and cable.  (From the FCC home page, http://www.ftp.gov/)")
(comment Statistics "The field of study concerned with the calculation, manipulation and interpretation of numerical data.")
(comment Anarchism "#$Anarchism is a political theory which posits the authority of government is unnecessary and not desirable.  A society in which people should freely associate and cooperate voluntarily is advocated.  A person who believes in #$Anarchism prefers #$Anarchy over any other system of government.")
(comment PeriodicTable "The #$PropositionalConceptualWork in which the chemical elements are represented according to their atomic numbers in a tabular form with information about each element in the cell of that element.  Columns and rows of the table have meanings associated with the normal electronic shell structure of the element.")
(comment Cribbage "A #$CardGame in which each player is dealt 6 cards and discards one or two to make up the crib")
(comment Pachinko "All instances of a game of Japanese pinball played on a vertical board")
(comment Charades "All instances of a game in which a player acts out a phrase for others to guess")
(comment AllFours-CardGame "All instances of card games in which points are won for taking the high or low or jack or game")
(comment Baccarat "The #$CardGame played in casinos in which two or more punters gamble against the banker; the player wins who holds 2 or 3 cards that total closest to nine")
(comment Blackjack-CardGame "The #$CardGame in which the object is to hold cards having a higher count than those dealt to the bank up to but not exceeding 21")
(comment Roulette "All instances of a gambling game in which players bet on which compartment of a revolving wheel a small ball will come to rest in")
(comment Bridge-TheGame "All instances of any of various card games based on whist for four players, the most common being contract bridge, a variety of bridge in which the bidder receives points toward game only if he takes at least the number of tricks bid.")
(comment Robotics "The field of study whose focus are applications of #$ArtificialIntelligence to action planning for and control of #$Robots.")
(comment Casino-TheGame "A #$CardGame in which cards face up on the table are taken with eligible cards in the hand")
(comment Ecarte-CardGame "A #$CardGame for 2 players; played with 32 cards and king high")
(comment Euchre "A #$CardGame similar to #$Ecarte-CardGame; each player is dealt 5 cards and the player making trump must take 3 tricks to win a hand")
(comment Fantan "All instances of a card game in which you play your sevens and other cards in sequence in the same suit as their sevens; you win if you are the first to use all your cards")
(comment Faro-TheGame "All instances of a card game in which players bet against the dealer on the cards he will draw from a dealing box")
(comment GoFish "A child's #$CardGame for two players who try to assemble books of cards by asking the opponent for particular cards")
(comment MonteGame "All instances of a gambling card game of Spanish origin; 3 or 4 cards are dealt face up and players bet that one of them will be matched before the others as the cards are dealt from the pack one at a time")
(comment Michigan-TheGame "A #$GamblingCardGame in which chips are placed on the ace and king and queen and jack of separate suits (taken from a separate deck); a player plays the lowest card of a suit in his hand and successively higher cards are played until the sequence stops; the player who plays a card matching one in the layout wins all the chips on that card")
(comment Pinochle "A #$CardGame played with a 48-card pack (two of each suit for high cards); play resembles whist")
(comment Piquet "All instances of a card game for two players using a reduced pack of 32 cards")
(comment DrawPoker "All instances of poker in which a player can discard cards and receive substitutes from the dealer")
(comment HighLowPoker "All instances of poker in which the high and low hands split the pot")
(comment StraightPoker "All instances of poker in which each player gets 5 cards face down and bets ar made without drawing any further cards")
(comment StripPoker "All instances of poker in which a player's losses are paid by removing an article of clothing")
(comment StudPoker "All instances of poker in which each player receives hole cards and the remainder are dealt face up; bets are placed after each card is dealt")
(comment RougeEtNoir-Gambling "All instances of a card game in which two rows of cards are dealt and players can bet on the color of the cards or on which row will have a count nearer some number. This is not the solitaire game")
(comment BoliviaGame "All instances of a form of canasta in which sequences can be melded")
(comment Samba-CardGame "All instances of a form of canasta using three decks and six jokers")
(comment HerbalMedicine "#$HerbalMedicine is a branch of #$AlternativeMedicine           that uses natural plant substances to treat illnesses. The           practice has existed since prehistoric times and is used         today by up to 80% of the world's population as a primary         form of medicine. Herbs may be used directly as teas (see           #$InfusionDrink) or extracts, or they may be used in the           production of drugs.")
(comment ProbabilityTheory "The mathematical study of probabilities.")
(comment Nutrition "The study of diet and its effects on health.")
(comment USRobotics "U.S. Robotics, the telecommunications company. It just merged with 3Com (1999).")
(comment PlateTectonics "The #$ScientificFieldOfStudy whose basic assumptions hold that the Earth's surface is fragmented into a dozen or more plates of various sizes, and that these #$TectonicPlates are moving relative to one another and sliding upon a hotter inner layer.")
(comment IranBiologicalWeaponReserve "This group denotes the set of biological weapons Iran possesses.")
(comment UnitedNationsOrganizationMt "This microtheory contains facts about the United Nations contained in the web pages found at http://www.un.org/.")
(comment Calculus "The subfield of #$Mathematics that studies continuously changing quantities (cf. #$quantityChangeFunction). The #$Calculus is characterized by the use of infinite processes, involving passage to a limit-the notion of tending toward, or approaching, an ultimate value. The English #$Physicist Isaac Newton and the German #$Mathematician G. W. Leibniz, working independently, developed the #$Calculus during #$TheSeventeenthCenturyAD.")
(comment Archaeology "the scientific study of historic or prehistoric peoples and their cultures by analysis of their artifacts, inscriptions, monuments, and other such remains, esp. those that have been excavated.")
(comment Eugenics "The study of improving the human race by encouraging the propagation of desirable genetic traits and discouraging the propagation of undesirable ones.")
(comment Kinesiology "the science dealing with the interrelationship of the physiological processes and anatomy of the human body with respect to movement.")
(comment Metaphysics "The subfield of #$Philosophy concerned with the ultimate nature of existence.")
(comment Meteorology "the science dealing with the atmosphere and its phenomena, including weather and climate.")
(comment Entomology "The subfield of #$Zoology dealing with #$Insects.")
(comment Etymology "The subfield of #$Linguistics that investigates the history, development, and origin of words.")
(comment Maoism "This belief system models the variant of communism that Mao Tse-Tung fleshed out for the Chinese context.")
(comment Creationism "the doctrine that matter and all things were created, substantially as they now exist, by an omnipotent Creator, and not gradually evolved or developed. See #$beliefStatements assertions on this constant for how this is represented in Cyc.")
(comment Kabbalah "A system of interpretation of the Jewish Scriptures based upon a tradition claimed to have been handed down orally from Abraham. Despite that claimed antiquity, the earliest known formulation dates from 11th century France. #$Kabbalah spread from there most notably to Spain. Most varieties of #$Kabbalah share the doctrines of emanation and the transmigration of souls, and the belief that every word, letter, number, and even accent in certain texts contains mysteries interpretable by those who know the secret. Typically, the names for God are believed to contain miraculous power and each letter of the divine name is considered potent.")
(comment SixPlusTwo-InternationalOrganization "Six plus Two is the ministerial group of #$Afghanistan's neighbors and #$Russia and the #$UnitedStatesOfAmerica.  They seek immediate cessation of #$Afghanistan's conflicts and the establishment of a broad-based, multi-ethnic, representative government.")
