(comment MereologyMt "The more general #$TheoryMicrotheory that contains reasonning about parts of #$Individual.")
(comment MereologyMt "The more general #$TheoryMicrotheory that contains reasonning about parts of instances of #$Individual. The theory axiomatized is sometimes called 'ground mereology' and is not considered to provide the minimal axiomatization of the notion of 'parthood'. There's two main direction in which to extend this basic mereology: on one hand, introducing supplementation principles [starts with #$MinimalMereologyMt], on the other hand, introducing closure principles and defining sum and product [starts with #$ClosedMereology]. These two directions may be combined. The set of possible extensions is axiomatisable in a lattice of microtheories which meet is #$MereologyMt. Preserving a basic sub-minimal mereology is motivated by the use that other kinds of extensions of mereology are making of some restricted set of mereological notions and principles, in particular there are mereotopolgical theories that do not have supplementation principles.")
(comment HumanPhysiologyVocabularyMt "The #$VocabularyMicrotheory for #$HumanPhysiologyMt")
(comment CycAdministrator "This constant is for use by Cyc administrators responsible for installation, setup and maintenance of Cyc images.")
(comment Phrenology "#$Phrenology is an attempt at theorizing in a scientific way the belief that the nature and the characteristic of mental faculties is strongly correlated to the shape of skull of individual. It has been initiated with the work of #$FranzJosephGall. After a period of great popularity in Europe it became out of fashion and eventually discredited as a science. Its interest rests in the elucidation of how modern theories of cerebral localization and neuroscience are rooted in such corpus of beliefs as early XIXth century phrenological theories. Especially, its influence needs to be linked to that of the later work of #$Pierre-PaulBroca.")
(comment LanguageAndWritingSystemVocabularyMt "The #$VocabularyMicrotheory for #$LanguageAndWritingSystemMt")
(comment WritingSystemCharactersVocabularyMt "The #$VocabularyMicrotheory for #$WritingSystemCharactersMt.")
(comment ComputerGVocabularyMt "The #$VocabularyMicrotheory for #$ComputerGMt.")
(comment ComputerSoftwareVocabularyMt "The #$VocabularyMicrotheory for #$ComputerSoftwareMt.")
(comment ComputerHardwareVocabularyMt "The #$VocabularyMicrotheory for #$ComputerHardwareMt.")
(comment PeopleVocabularyMt "The #$VocabularyMicrotheory for #$PeopleMt.")
(comment BiologicalSocialVocabularyMt "The #$VocabularyMicrotheory for #$BiologicalSocialMt.")
(comment SituationPredicateRepresentationMt "This microtheory stores basic definitional information about constants used for \"non-Davidsonian\" representation of situations, events and actions -- notably, it stores the definitions of #$SituationPredicate and it's specializations, #$EventPredicate and #$ActionPredicate (qq.v.). If you use situation predicates of any kind, your work should go in some microtheory that is a specialization of #$SituationPredicateRepresentationMt. These predicates allow Cyc to represent situations without positing the existence of situation particulars: instead of asserting (#$thereExists ?X (#$and (#$isa ?X #$Running) (#$performedBy ?X #$BritneySpears))), we make a statement having the same form as `Britney runs', e.g., (runs BritneySpears) or (#$does #$Running #$BritneySpears) -- notice the absence of existential quantifiers ranging over events in the latter representation. #$SituationPredicateRepresentationMt is the topmost microtheory for situation-predicate representation: _all_and_only_ domain-specific representation using #$SituationPredicates and the like should be placed in microtheories _below_  this one. Note further that conversion rules between situation-predicate and Davidsonian assertions (like the #$thereExists example above) should _not_ be asserted in this microtheory, but rather in some restricted context that is a specialization of #$Davidsonian-NonDavidsonian-ConversionMt (q.v.).")
(comment UnitedStatesNaturalGeographyVocabularyMt "The #$VocabularyMicrotheory for #$UnitedStatesNaturalGeographyMt.")
(comment Corsica-TheIsland "The Corsica island,  roughly the territory of #$Corsica.")
(comment RCCVocabularyMt "The #$VocabularyMicrotheory for #$RCCMt.")
(comment OrganizationGVocabularyMt "This is the #$VocabularyMicrotheory for #$OrganizationGMt.")
(comment OrganizationGMt "This is a microtheory that contains information about organizations in a very broad context, a context which could be usefully applied across a wide variety of situations and which is not dependent upon any particular location of a specific #$Organization or type of #$Organization.  More specific axioms and vocabulary about #$Organizations are contained in #$OrganizationMt and its spec-microtheories.")
(comment ComputerHardwareDataVocabularyMt "The #$VocabularyMicrotheory for #$ComputerHardwareDataMt.")
(comment TheoryOfScienceMt "A microtheory that contains the axiomatization of terms used to describe science and scientific theories.")
(comment TheoryOfScienceVocabularyMt "The #$VocabularyMicrotheory for #$TheoryOfScienceMt.")
(comment HistoryOfScienceDataMt "A #$DataMicrotheory used to record historical knowledge about particular sciences and scientific theories.")
(comment MilitaryGMt "The most general #$Microtheory for defining anything military-related.  This is the place to put any military-specific assertions that you may be tempted to place in #$BaseKB.  Most general military-related assertions belong in #$ModernMilitaryVehiclesMt, #$ModernMilitaryWeaponsMt, #$MilitaryForceStructureMt, or their specs (including #$ModernMilitaryMt).  If a term or assertion is needed in more than one of these three mts, then place it in #$ModernMilitaryMt.  No assertion in this #$Microtheory should reference any specific #$TemporalThing, such as a specific country or military force, nor should it reference any ``brand name'' device (such as V-2 rocket).  Note this is not a \"modern\" military #$Microtheory.")
(comment MilitaryGVocabularyMt "The #$VocabularyMicrotheory for #$MilitaryGMt.")
(comment ParaphraseVocabularyMt "The #$VocabularyMicrotheory for #$ParaphraseMt.")
(comment ParaphraseMt "Contains knowledge specific to the domain of paraphrasing CycL expressions into natural language expressions, but not specific to any one natural language.")
(comment AgentGVocabularyMt "The #$VocabularyMicrotheory for #$AgentGMt.")
(comment EcologyMt "This microtheory is for the axiomatization of knowledge of #$Ecology. It deals with the representation of relations between organisms and their physical and geographical environments.")
(comment CyclishParaphraseMt "This is the context used for generating Cyclish paraphrases for CycL expressions. It is all right for paraphrase assertions in this context to result in paraphrases with explicit references to CycL terms.")
(comment causalActors "(#$causalActors EVENT ACTOR) means that ACTOR  played a causal role in bringing about EVENT.")
(comment MilitaryTermsVocabularyMt "A need has been expressed for having a set of military terms to be visible from #$PlanningVocabularyMt and #$ExpansionVocabularyMt, without having the assertions in #$HumanActivitiesMt available.  Until a #$VocabularyMicrotheory has been established for #$HumanActivitiesMt #$MilitaryTermsVocabularyMt will be used for that purpose.  There has been a temptation to place military constants (even extremely application specific ones such as #$BPV-BlockParty5ConflictEvent) in #$BaseKB because of the lack of this microtheory.  Now that #$MilitaryTermsVocabularyMt has been created, all military terms may migrate out of #$BaseKB.")
(comment needsToDo "This predicate relates an agent to a type of event s/he needs to do in order to achieve or further some (unspecified) goal  (as in \"Pam needs to buy some groceries\").  The intended sense of  `needs' includes, but is not limited to, matters of life and death; see the comment on #$needs-Generic for a fuller account. (#$needsToDo AGENT EVENTTYPE) means that AGENT needs to fill the  #$doneBy actor slot (or some specialization thereof) with respect  to an (indeterminate, future) instance of EVENTTYPE.  Calling the  instance \"indeterminate\" reflects an assumption that there is no  one particular instance of EVENTTYPE such that AGENT needs to do _it_.   (To assume otherwise would be highly dubious.  For it is far from  obvious that there is any meaningful sense in which future events  exist in the present; and even if they do, should AGENT's need go  unfulfilled there might not even be any _future_ instance of  EVENTTYPE that could be the one that she needed to do.)  Rather,  AGENT's need could be satisfied by her being the \"doer\" in _any_  future instance of EVENTTYPE, modulo whatever practical constraints  are imposed on AGENT by her circumstances.  (E.g. Pam must buy those  groceries within the next few days or it will be too late.)  What  AGENT needs to do an EVENTTYPE for (i.e. what goal of hers it would  further) and what will happen to AGENT if she fails to do so  (e.g. whether she will die, suffer an important loss, or just be  noticeably inconvenienced) are left unspecified in the above formula,  though in practice they might be inferable from other assertions in  the context (see e.g. #$needsFor).  Note that, as with the other  specializations of #$needs-Generic, #$needsToDo has to do with a  more-or-less \"objective\" requirement of AGENT's to do an EVENTTYPE,  and does not entail a corresponding \"felt need\" on the part of AGENT.   (E.g. Pam herself might not realize that her cupboards are bare.)   A felt need of this sort is perhaps best represented as an assertion  that a given agent both #$knows that s/he objectively #$needsToDo a  certain type of event and #$desires that s/he do so.  See also the  other specializations of #$needs-Generic.")
(comment ArtifactGMt "This #$TheoryMicrotheory is used for the axiomatization of #$Artifact-Generic. This is the most general microtheory that discusses this topic.")
(comment ArtifactGVocabularyMt "The #$VocabularyMicrotheory for #$ArtifactGMt.")
(comment FixedStructureMt "This #$TheoryMicrotheory is used to axiomatize knowledge about #$FixedStructure. This is the most general microtheory that discusses this notion.")
(comment FixedStructureVocabularyMt "The #$VocabularyMicrotheory for #$FixedStructureMt.")
(comment InterAgentArtifactMt "The microtheory with the most general assertions linking types of agents with types of artifacts.")
(comment CultureGMt "The #$TheoryMicrotheory for describing types and features of cultures (cf. #$Culture-Practice).  Instances of cultures belong in #$PeopleMt (if real #$HumanCultures) or specialized mts for fictional cultures or cultures of non-human animals.")
(comment GeographicalRegionGMt "The microtheory for defining #$GeographicalThing, #$GeographicalRegion, and the intersections of these with #$IntangibleIndividual and #$PartiallyTangible.  The concepts of latitude, longitude, rotational poles and axes are included, but #$TopographicalFeatures are described in the more specialized #$NaturalGeographyMt, and political regions are defined in #$GeographyMt.")
(comment GeographicalRegionGVocabularyMt "The #$VocabularyMicrotheory for #$GeographicalRegionGMt.")
(comment ProductUsageMt "The #$Microtheory for describing the use of products by animals (including humans).")
(comment ProductUsageVocabularyMt "The #$VocabularyMicrotheory for #$ProductUsageMt.")
(comment GeometryGVocabularyMt "The #$VocabularyMicrotheory for #$GeometryGMt.")
(comment ClothingGMt "The #$GeneralMicrotheory describing non-brandname clothing.  Brandname clothing is defined in #$BrandNameProductGMt.")
(comment PharmaceuticalUseVocabularyMt "The #$VocabularyMicrotheory for #$PharmaceuticalUseMt.")
(comment SpatialGMt "The #$GeneralMicrotheory for describing the most general spatial vocabulary.  Terms in #$SpatialGMt are generally topologically invariant.  The specMt #$GeometryGMt is used for geometrical shapes, angles, properties.  Other spatial terms are described in #$NaiveSpatialMt.")
(comment SpatialGVocabularyMt "The #$VocabularyMicrotheory for #$SpatialGMt.")
(comment PathSystemsVocabularyMt "The #$VocabularyMicrotheory for #$PathSystemsMt.")
(comment WorldPoliticalGeographyDataMt "The #$DataMicrotheory that describes purely agentive features of instances of #$GeopoliticalEntity whose territory is on the #$PlanetEarth.  Any assertions that are also related to spatial concepts as well belongs in #$WorldGeographyMt or #$WorldGeographyDualistMt as appropriate.")
(comment WorldPoliticalGeographyDataVocabularyMt "The #$VocabularyMicrotheory for #$WorldPoliticalGeographyDataMt.")
