(comment CBLCommandHavingSucceed "This is the collection of #$CBLCommands that succeed immediately after sending a message.")
(comment CBLCommandIssuingMessages "This is the collection of #$CycBehaviorLanguage command events in which a message is issued.")
(comment CBLMessageDispatchingPriority-Low "This is the collection of #$CBLCommands that issue messages at low dispatching priority.")
(comment assertHasBeenReviewedBy "<code>(#$assertHasBeenReviewedBy ASRT AGENT)</code> holds just in case ASRT has been evaluated by AGENT in some sweep of KB content.")
(comment cblReplyWithSessionId "(cblReplyWithSessionId MESSAGE-ISSUING-COMMAND EXPRESSION) means that #$CycBehaviorLanguage MESSAGE-ISSUING-COMMAND has EXPRESSION to be evaluated and included in the message.  The receiver uses the parameter value GUID in the in-reply-to slot of the reply message so that session variables can be restored when the message is processed subsequently processed.  See #$cblInReplyToSessionId.")
(comment CBLMessageDispatchingPriority-High "This is the collection of #$CBLCommands that issue messages at high dispatching priority.")
(comment cblReplyWith "(cblReplyWith MESSAGE-ISSUING-COMMAND EXPRESSION) means that #$CycBehaviorLanguage MESSAGE-ISSUING-COMMAND has EXPRESSION to be evaluated and included in the message.  The receiver uses the parameter value in the in-reply-to slot of the reply message.  See #$cblInReplyTo.")
(comment SecondsSince1970-Indexical "This term denotes, the number of seconds that have passed since the start of 1970.  This is calculated by determining the time elapsed (via #$TimeElapsedFn) from the start of 1970 until now and converting the resulting time into seconds.  Note that the value of this term increases by one as each second passes.  Also note that this does not denote a #$Time-Quantity.  Rather, it denotes a positive integer.")
(comment CBLSensation "This is the collection of #$CycBehaviorLanguage sensation command events.")
(comment cblSensationName "(cblSensationName SENSATION-COMMAND PROGRAM-IDENTIFIER) means that within #$CycBehaviorLanguage, PROGRAM-IDENTIFIER provides the sensation name for the SENSATION-COMMAND to handle.")
(comment cblInreplyToConversationId "(cblInreplyToConversationId SEND-COMMAND EXPRESSION) means that CycBehaviorLanguage SEND-COMMAND has EXPRESSION to be evaluated and included in the message. The receiver uses the parameter value GUID in the in-reply-to-conversation-id slot of the reply message so that the message can be associated with the thread that is waiting for a reply in the parent node.")
(comment cblInReplyToSessionId "(cblInReplyToSessionId COMMAND EXPRESSION) means that #$CycBehaviorLanguage COMMAND has EXPRESSION to be evaluated and included in the message.  The receiver uses the parameter value GUID in the in-reply-to-session-id slot of the reply message so that the corresponding session variables can be restored in the parent node when the message is handled.")
(comment CBLEndSession "This is the collection of #$CycBehaviorLanguage end-session command events.")
(comment CBLBeginSession "This is the collection of #$CycBehaviorLanguage begin-session command events.")
(comment CBLSessionStateVariable "This is the collection of #$CycBehaviorLanguage session state variables.")
(comment cblNodeSessionStateVariable "(cblNodeSessionStateVariable NODE-CLASS SESSION-VARIABLE) means that #$CycBehaviorLanguage NODE-CLASS has SESSION-VARIABLE as one of its session variables.")
(comment CBLNodeAddressFn "(CBLNodeAddressFn NODE-ADDRESS-STRING) denotes the unique node address in the #$CycBehaviorEngine corresponding to the NODE-ADDRESS-STRING.")
(comment CharniakParser "A parser for English developed at #$BrownUniversity by Eugene Charniak.")
(comment cblHasParentNode "(cblNodeHasParent CYC-BEHAVIOR-LANGUAGE-CHILD CYC-BEHAVIOR-LANGUAGE-NODE-PARENT) means that CYC-BEHAVIOR-LANGUAGE-CHILD has as its parent CYC-BEHAVIOR-LANGUAGE-NODE-PARENT.  All #$CycBehaviorLanguage nodes except the top-most have a parent node.  All #$CycBehaviorLanguage actuators, sensors and actuator-sensors have a parent node.")
(comment cblHasNextSiblingNode "(cblHasNextSiblingNode NODE-1 NODE-2) means that NODE-1 has as its next sibling NODE-2.  The last child node of a parent node does not have a next sibling.  If a node is the only child node of a parent node then it also does not have a next sibling.")
(comment cblInheritNodeTypeCharacteristicsFromNodeTypes "(cblInheritNodeTypeCharacteristicsFromNodeTypes NODE-TYPE NODE-TYPE-LIST) means that #$CycBehaviorLanguage #$CBLNodes of the type NODE-TYPE inherit the variables and methods from the #$CBLNode types in the NODE-TYPE-LIST, if they are not already defined by name in the NODE-TYPE or in preceding node types in the list.")
(comment CBLNode "This is the specialization of #$CycAgent that define #$CycBehaviorEngine nodes that organize Cyc behavior via an associated set of  #$CBLMethods.  Although a node definition may result in multiple instantiated executing nodes, this group is considered an individual with respect to the node definition.")
(comment CBLNodeAndSubordinatesOrganizationFn "(CBLNodeAndSubordinatesOrganizationFn NODE) denotes the #$Organization formed by the executing #$CycBehaviorEngine nodes defined by NODE and its direct and indirect subordinate nodes.")
(comment cblDocumentation "(cblDocumentation CYC-BEHAVIOR-LANGUAGE-OBJECT STRING) means that STRING provides human-readable documentation for the CYC-BEHAVIOR-LANGUAGE-OBJECT.  A node is created with documentation and seeks to elaborate its equivalent #$missionOfOrganization proposition, and to populate its #$institutionalFocus assertion.")
(comment CBLNodeOrganization "The tree structured organization whose members are #$CBLNodes running in the the #$CycBehaviorEngine.")
(comment CBLNodeOrganizationalChart "The tree structured organization chart depicting the command hierarchy of the #$CBLNodes running in the #$CycBehaviorEngine.")
(comment DoraNeighborhood-Beirut-Lebanon "A neighborhood in Beirut, Lebanon")
(comment BekaaValley-Lebanon "A fertile valley in Lebanon that extends into Syria, located roughly 30 km (around 19 mi) east of Beirut.  This constant represents the Lebanon part of the valley.")
(comment BekaaValley "A fertile valley in Lebanon that extends into Syria, located roughly 30 km (around 19 mi) east of Beirut.  This constant represents the  whole valley.  See #$BekaaValley-Lebanon for the representation of the Lebanon part of the valley.")
(comment MarjounDistrict-Lebanon "An Administrative district of Lebanon")
(comment Al-Qubbah-Neighborhood-Tripoli-Lebanon "A neighborhood in Tripoli, Lebanon")
(comment cblTaskFromHumanCyclist "(cblTaskFromHumanCyclist ASSIGNED-TASK PARAMETER-VALUES-LIST HUMAN-CYCLIST) means that the HUMAN-CYCLIST has assigned the ASSIGNED-TASK to Cyc via the #$CycBehaviorEngine with the given PARAMETER-VALUES-LIST.")
(comment cblActuatorForTask "(cblActuatorForTask CYC-BEHAVIOR-LANGUAGE-ACTUATOR PROGRAM-IDENTIFIER) means that CYC-BEHAVIOR-LANGUAGE-ACTUATOR performs the action type identified by PROGRAM-IDENTIFIER.")
(comment CBLBuiltIn_ExecutionMt "This is the collection of #$CycBehaviorLanguage built-in state variables that hold the execution microtheory of the #$CBLNode.")
(comment CBLBuiltIn_PoolThreadActivity "This is the collection of #$CycBehaviorLanguage built-in state variables that hold a string describing the activity of executor pool threads.")
(comment CBLMethodHavingInheritSubtasks "This is the collection of #$CBLMethods that inherit subtasks from superclass methods of the same name.")
(comment CBLMethodHavingOverrideSubtasks "This is the collection of #$CBLMethods that override subtasks from superclass methods of the same name.")
(comment CBLTaskCommandHavingWait "This is the collection of #$CBLTasks that wait for the sent task to succeed or fail, and optionally bind results.")
(comment CBLTaskCommandHavingNoWait "This is the collection of #$CBLTasks that do not for the sent task to succeed or fail, but rather immediately proceed to the next command.")
(comment cblAssertionStrength "(cblAssertionStrengthn ASSERT-COMMAND STRENGTH) means that #$CycBehaviorLanguage ASSERT-COMMAND has STRENGTH as the justification strength (e.g. #$Monotonic-JustificationStrength or #$Default-JustificationStrength.) of its assertion.")
(comment CBLBuiltIn_ChildNodeNames "This is the collection of #$CycBehaviorLanguage built-in state variables that hold the names of the child nodes of the #$CBLNode.")
(comment introducesTo "A ternary #$CotemporalPredicate that is used to state that an agent introduces a second agent to a third agent.  <code>(#$introducesTo PRESENTER PRESENTEE PRESENTED-TO)</code> means that <code>PRESENTER</code> introduces <code>PRESENTEE</code> to <code>PRESENTED-TO</code>.  
<p>
Note that the above does <i>not</i> entail that <code>(#$introducesTo PRESENTER PRESENTED-TO PRESENTEE)</code>, though it strongly suggests it, since in most social circumstances two agents normally get introduced to each other at the same (or nearly the same) time.  
<p>
It is assumed that, just prior to the introduction, <code>PRESENTER</code> is mutually acquainted with both of the other parties, and <code>PRESENTED-TO</code> is not acquainted with <code>PRESENTEE</code>.  (<code>PRESENTEE</code> might conceivably be already acquainted with <code>PRESENTED-TO</code>, as the latter might already have been introduced to the former.)  Just after the introduction, of course, <code>PRESENTED-TO</code> <i>is</i> acquainted with <code>PRESENTEE</code>.")
(comment OrgToOrgRolePredicate "A subcollection of #$BinaryPredicate.  #$OrgToOrgRolePredicate includes only those instances of #$BinaryPredicate that can relate an organization to an organization.")
(comment ProblemSolvingMt "A type of microtheory which holds a theory about 'problem solving', that is actions which manipulate plans, either by human or artificial agents. This would typically be inherited into other Mts in which agents may do problem solving, e.g. HumanActivitiesMt. Note that 'problem solving' is a term of art, and is not the same thing as SolvingAProblem. The latter may involve actions in the former, however.")
(comment IntelligenceAnalysisTask-TemplateTopic "This is a template topic type for describing intelligence analysis tasks.")
(comment CurrentWorldDataCollectorMt-NonHomocentric "The #$Microtheory which collects together all of the instances of #$GeneralMicrotheory (including instances of #$DataMicrotheory) as #$genlMts (excepting #$HomoCentricNaturalismMt), so that any scenario or project microtheory which needs wide access to the KB but needs to accept either non-human #$Persons or supernatural things can use this as a #$genlMt and be assured that all generally defined terms are available.  If no need exists to access instances of #$TemporalThing, #$WorldLikeOursCollectorMt-NonHomocentric should be used instead.  If for the purposes of the project all persons are human and no supernatural things are being reasoned about use the #$CollectorMicrotheory #$CurrentWorldDataCollectorMt or #$WorldLikeOursCollectorMt.")
(comment AliBabaKSMappingConstant "The collection of constants used to map EDB Ali Baba format databases.")
(comment mediumAmountOf "The instance of #$GenericValuePredicate (q.v.) that corresponds to (see #$correspondingGenericValuePredicate) the generic-value function #$MediumAmountFn.  <code>(#$mediumAmountOf QUANTTYPE QUANTITY)</code> holds just in case <code>QUANTITY</code> is the #$GenericQuantity that corresponds to a <i>medium amount</i> of <code>QUANTTYPE</code>.
<p>
For example, <code>(#$mediumAmountOf #$Happiness (#$MediumAmountFn #$Happiness))</code> holds.  And <code>(#$levelOfCompatibility OLDTHING NEWTHING #$mediumAmountOf)</code> holds just in case <code>OLDTHING</code> and <code>NEWTHING</code> are compatible to a medium degree.")
(comment PartiallyTangibleTypeByNonNumericSize "A subcollection of #$PartiallyTangibleTypeByPhysicalFeature.  These 'size' features
do not consist of actual measurements.  Instead, they are generally
collections of things with a comparable size.  The collections are used to represent a size along with a dimension and can be used to represent sizes of objects whose sizes do not come in dense degrees.  The sizes of ice cream cones available at your local soda fountain is an example of sizes along a dimension that don't typically admit of dense degrees.  On the other hand, the sizes of lengths of string typically do admit of dense degrees.  Instances include: #$TwoLanesOrWiderRoadway, #$CupSize-C, #$Skirt-MiniLength, etc..")
(comment MuscleTenseness "A collection of particular quantities of muscle tenseness.  Instances of this collection can be created with the generic value functions.  See #$GenericValueFunction.  For instance, (#$hasPhysiologicalQuantity  Bob (#$MediumAmountFn #$MuscleTenseness)) holds just in case Bob's muscles are moderately tense, perhaps from too much exercise.")
(comment TheYear2000 "The year 2000.")
(comment ArtificialIntelligence "The field of artificial intelligence.")
