(comment (ist UniversalVocabularyMt (isa PumpingGasEvent TemporalStuffType)) "In any timeslice of a #$PumpingGasEvent above a certain level of granularity (the time it takes a drop of gasoline to go from the pump to the tank), *some* portion of gasoline translates from the pump to the tank. Therefore this collection is an instance of #$TemporalStuffType.")
(comment (ist BaseKB (not (genlPreds endingDate endsDuring))) "#$endingDate is reflexive, whereas #$endsDuring is irreflexive and so does not hold between temporal things that end at the same time.  Thus #$endsDuring is not a generalization of #$endingDate.")
(comment (ist BaseKB (not (disjointWith TransitiveBinaryPredicate AntiTransitiveBinaryPredicate))) "Although few if any reified predicates should be instances of both #$TransitiveBinaryPredicate and #$AntiTransitiveBinaryPredicate, note that these collections are not strictly disjoint. Since both collections are defined with respect to the condition (on a predicate PRED) that both (PRED THING1 THING2) and (PRED THING2 THING3) hold (for a given THING1 - THING3), any predicate for which there are no three things satisfying this condition will (trivially) belong to both collections. For a (somewhat artificial) example, if restricted to the members of a society in which no one had more than two children, a predicate `youngerSiblingOf' would qualify as both transitive and anti-transitive. Similarly, a binary predicate with an necessarily empty extension is both transitive and anti-transitive. Still, it is not clear what practical reason there could be to individually represent any such predicate in the Cyc Knowledge Base.")
(comment (ist LogicalTruthMt (quotedIsa Quote CycLExpression)) "This assertion is meant to reflect the syncategorematic nature of `#$Quote' with respect to the CycL grammar.  The implementation-specific features of `#$Quote' are axiomatized in the #$LogicalTruthImplementationMt.")
(comment (ist LogicalTruthMt (quotedIsa EscapeQuote CycLExpression)) "This assertion is meant to reflect the syncategorematic nature of `#$EscapeQuote' with respect to the CycL grammar.  The implementation-specific features of `#$EscapeQuote' are axiomatized in the #$LogicalTruthImplementationMt.")
(comment (ist LogicalTruthImplementationMt (argQuotedIsa Quote 1 CycLTerm)) "This assertion might easily give the impression that #$Quote is really just the identity function on CycL terms.

For an explanation as to why this is _not_ in fact the case, see the comment on #$Quote and the shared #$NoteAboutQuotingInCycL.  These comments explain why this #$argIsa assertion is in the #$LogicalTruthImplementationMt instead of the #$LogicalTruthMt.")
(comment (ist BaseKB (not (genlPreds assertionAssertTime termCreationTime))) "Although #$termCreationTime and #$assertionAssertTime may be the same for most #$CycLAssertions, the former should not be a #$genlPreds of the latter.  This is because some assertions may have been deduced at a time *before* they were directly asserted by a #$HumanCyclist.")
(comment (ist BaseKB (not (genlPreds reformulatorEquals equals))) "Reformulation is not necessarily truth-preserving, and equality is.")
(comment (ist BaseKB (not (genls Agent-Generic CompositeTangibleAndIntangibleObject))) "#$Agent-Generic may include purely #$IntangibleIndividuals such as spirits, so it is not s spec of #$CompositeTangibleAndIntangibleObject")
